    "p": 28,
    "t": "Flipflops:\n➢ A flip-flop is a basic sequential logic circuit used to store one bit\nof binary information.\n➢ A flipflop has two stable states: Q=0 and Q`=1, So, it is also called\na bistable device.\n➢ Unlike a latch, a flip-flop is usually edge-triggered, meaning its\noutput changes only at the triggering edge of the clock signal.\nDifference Between Latches and Flip-Flops:\nS. No.\nLatches\nFlip-Flops\nA latch is a level-sensitive\nstorage element.\nA\nflip-flop\nis\nan\nedge-\ntriggered storage element.\nOutput\nchanges\nwhen\nthe\nenable/clock level is active.\nOutput changes only at the\nclock edge.\nIt is controlled by an enable\nsignal.\nIt is controlled by a clock\nsignal.\nIt can change output as long as\nenable is active.\nIt changes output only at\npositive or negative clock\nedge.\nIt is simpler in construction.\nIt is more complex than a\nlatch.\nIt may be affected by input\nchanges during enable period.\nIt is less affected by input\nchanges\nbetween\nclock\nedges.\nIt is faster but less controlled.\nIt\nprovides\nbetter\ntiming\ncontrol.\nIt\ndoes\nnot\nrequire\nedge\ntriggering.\nIt requires edge triggering.\nUsed for temporary storage and\nsimple memory circuits.\nUsed in registers, counters,\nshift\nregisters,\nand\nsynchronous circuits.\nExamples: SR latch, D latch.\nExamples: SR flip-flop, D\nflip-flop, JK flip-flop, T flip-\nflop.",
