    "p": 22,
    "t": "Computer Organization and Architecture\nUNIT-I: MCQs\nBoolean Algebra, Logic Gates, Binary Codes, Data Representation and Digital Computers\n1. The radix of the binary number system is:\n(a) 2\n(b) 8\n(c) 10\n(d) 16\nAnswer: (a) 2\n2. The decimal equivalent of 101012 is:\n(a) 19\n(b) 20\n(c) 21\n(d) 22\nAnswer: (c) 21\n3. The binary equivalent of decimal number 37 is:\n(a) 1001012\n(b) 1010012\n(c) 1100012\n(d) 1001112\nAnswer: (a) 1001012\n4. The BCD representation of decimal number 59\nis:\n(a) 0101 1001\n(b) 1011 1001\n(c) 0101 1010\n(d) 0110 1001\nAnswer: (a) 0101 1001\n5. In BCD, the combinations from 1010 to 1111\nare:\n(a) Valid codes\n(b) Invalid codes\n(c) Gray codes\n(d) ASCII codes\nAnswer: (b) Invalid codes\n6. A code in which only one bit changes between\nsuccessive values is:\n(a) BCD code\n(b) Excess-3 code\n(c) Gray code\n(d) ASCII code\nAnswer: (c) Gray code\n7. The Gray code equivalent of binary number\n10112 is:\n(a) 1110\n(b) 1101\n(c) 1010\n(d) 1001\nAnswer: (a) 1110\n8. The binary equivalent of Gray code 1110 is:\n(a) 1011\n(b) 1001\n(c) 1101\n(d) 1111\nAnswer: (a) 1011\n9. Excess-3 code of decimal digit 6 is:\n(a) 0110\n(b) 1001\n(c) 1010\n(d) 0011\nAnswer: (b) 1001\n10. ASCII code is mainly used to represent:\n(a) Arithmetic operations\n(b) Characters\n(c) Logic gates\n(d) Memory hierarchy\nAnswer: (b) Characters\n11. One flip-flop can store:\n(a) One bit\n(b) One byte\n(c) Four bits\n(d) Eight bits\nAnswer: (a) One bit\n12. A register containing 16 flip-flops can store:\n(a) 8 bits\n(b) 16 bits\n(c) 32 bits\n(d) 64 bits\nAnswer: (b) 16 bits\n13. A group of flip-flops used to store binary infor-\nmation is called:\n(a) Decoder\n(b) Encoder\n(c) Register\n(d) Multiplexer\nAnswer: (c) Register\n14. The register that holds the address of the next\ninstruction is:\n(a) Accumulator\n(b) Program Counter",
