    "p": 16,
    "t": "EXAMPLE\nMEANING\nSYMBOL\nNAME\np: “It is raining”\n¬p: “It is not raining”\n“NOT p”; reverses the truth value of\nproposition p\n¬p\nNegation\np: “User is logged in”\nq: “Password is correct”\np ∧q: “User is logged in AND password\nis correct”\n“p AND q”; true only if both p and q are\ntrue\np ∧q\nConjunction\np: “System is online”\nq: “Backup is available”\np ∨q: “System is online OR backup is\navailable”\n“p OR q”; true if at least one of p or q is\ntrue\np ∨q\nDisjunction\np: “It rains”\nq: “Roads are wet”\np →q: “If it rains, then roads are wet”\n“If p then q”; false only when p is true and\nq is false\np →q\nConditional\np: “Number is even”\nq: “Number is divisible by 2” p\nq\n“p if and only if q”; true when p and q have\nthe same truth value\np\nq\nBicondition\nal\nLOGICAL CONNECTIVES (USED TO FORM COMPOUND PROPOSITIONS)\nNegation reverses the truth value of a proposition.\nLet p be a proposition. The negation of p denoted by ¬p is the statement “It is not the case that p”\nExample:\nTruth Table: It is a table displaying the truth values of the propositions.\nNEGATION (¬)\n¬p\np\nF\nT\nT\nF\n¬p\np\n¬p: “It is not raining”\np: “It is raining”\n¬p: “The file does not exist”\np: “The file exists”\n¬p: “The light is OFF”\np: “The light is ON”\n¬p: “The user is not authenticated”\np: “The user is authenticated”\n¬p: “5 is not a prime number”\np: “5 is a prime number”\nA conjunctive proposition is a compound proposition formed by joining two propositions\nusing AND (∧).\np ∧q is true only if both p and q are true.\n“Both conditions must be satisfied.”\nUsed in programming when multiple conditions must hold.\nIf any one condition fails, the whole conjunction becomes false.\nCONJUNCTION (∧)\np ∧q\nq\np\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\nF\n•“I will attend the exam AND submit the assignment.”\n•“The light is ON AND the fan is ON.”\n•“The door is closed AND locked.”\nComputer Science Examples\nUser authentication:\nUser is logged in AND password is correct\nRange checking:\nx > 0 AND x < 100\nA disjunctive proposition is formed using OR (∨).\n𝑝∨𝑞is true if at least one of p or q is true.\nIt is true when at least one of the propositions is true.\n“At least one condition must hold.” This is inclusive OR.\nDISJUNCTION (∨)\np ∨q\nq\np\nT\nT\nT\nT\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nF\n•“Today is Saturday OR Sunday.”\n•“You may pay by cash OR card.”\n•“The alarm rings if there is smoke OR fire.”\nComputer Science Examples\nServer availability:\nMain server is running OR backup server is\nrunning\nLogin options:\nLogin using email OR mobile number",
    "ocr": false
