    "p": 18,
    "t": "Everyday Examples\n“You can enter the room if and only if you\nhave an ID card.”\n“A number is even iff it is divisible by 2.”\n“The bulb glows iff the switch is ON.”\nMathematically:\n𝒑\n𝒒≡𝒑→𝒒∧𝒒→𝒑\nExam Eligibility Example\nLet:\np = “A student has attendance ≥ 75%”\nq = “The student is allowed to write the\nexam”\nStatement:\n“A student is allowed to write the exam if and\nonly if attendance is at least 75%.”\nCase 1: p = T, q = T\nAttendance ≥ 75%, student allowed\nRule followed →Statement TRUE\nCase 2: p = T, q = F\nAttendance ≥ 75%, but student NOT allowed\nRule violated →FALSE\nCase 3: p = F, q = T\nAttendance < 75%, but student allowed\nRule violated →FALSE\nCase 4: p = F, q = F\nAttendance < 75%, student NOT allowed\nRule followed →TRUE\nNotice: both true or both false →true\nMathematical Definition Example (Very\nImportant)\nStatement:\n“An integer n is even if and only if n is divisible\nby 2.”\nWhy this is biconditional:\nIf n is even →divisible by 2\nIf divisible by 2 →n is even\nBoth directions hold — hence biconditional.\nProgramming Example (Conceptual)\nLet:\np = “Password is correct”\nq = “User is authenticated”\nSystem rule:\n“User is authenticated if and only if password is\ncorrect.”\nThis ensures:\nCorrect password →access granted\nAccess granted →password must have been correct\nThis prevents security loopholes.\nBiconditional (p\nq)\nImplication (p →q)\nFeature\nTwo-way\nOne-way\nDirection\nNecessary + sufficient\nSufficient only\nConditions\nTwo\nOne\nFalse cases\nStronger\nWeaker\nStrength\n(p ∧q) →r\nIf both p and q are true, then r is true\np →(q ∨r)\nIf p is true, then at least one of q or r is true\n(p\nq) ∧r\np and q are equivalent and r is true\nTELL ME WHAT THIS MEANS",
