    "p": 19,
    "t": "Exclusive OR (XOR) is a logical connective that is true only when exactly one of the\nstatements is true, but false when both are true or both are false. The word exclusive means\n“one or the other, but not both.”\nSymbolically, XOR is written as:\n𝑝⊕𝑞\nIn everyday language, the word “or” is often used in an exclusive sense:\n“You may take tea or coffee”\n(Usually means one, not both)\nHowever, in logic, OR (∨) is inclusive, meaning both can be true.\nXOR was introduced to capture this exactly-one condition, which inclusive OR cannot express.\nEXCLUSIVE OR (XOR)\np ⊕q\nq\np\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nF\nA proposition p ⊕q is true if and only if:\np is true and q is false, or\np is false and q is true\nIn all other cases, it is false.\nXOR can be expressed using basic connectives:\n𝑝⊕𝑞≡𝑝∨𝑞∧¬ 𝑝∧𝑞\nMeaning:\nAt least one is true\nBut not both\nThis equivalence is important in proofs and circuit design.\nMeaning\nConcept\nTRUE when same\np\nq\nTRUE when different\np ⊕q\nThe NAND operation is the negation of conjunction (AND).\nSymbolically:\n𝑝↑𝑞 or ¬ 𝑝∧𝑞\nIn words:\nNAND is false only when both inputs are true; otherwise it is true.\nNAND (NOT-AND)\nNAND: ¬(p ∧q)\np ∧q\nq\np\nF\nT\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\nThe NOR operation is the negation of disjunction (OR).\nSymbolically:\n𝑝↓𝑞 or ¬ 𝑝∨𝑞\nIn words:\nNOR is true only when both inputs are false; otherwise it is false.\nNOR (NOT-OR)\nNOR: ¬(p ∨q)\np ∨q\nq\np\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF",
