    "t": "For p →q:\nConverse: q →p\nInverse: ¬p →¬q\nContrapositive: ¬q →¬p\nThe contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original implication.\nCONVERSE, INVERSE, CONTRAPOSITIVE\nA tautology is a compound proposition that is always true, regardless of the truth values of its\nconstituent propositional variables.\nExample                                                                           𝑝∨¬𝑝\n1. TAUTOLOGY\np ∨¬p\n¬p\np\nT\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nResult\np∧q\nq\np\nT\nT\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\n(p∧q)→p\nAlways true → Tautology\nExample 3\n𝑝→𝑞∨𝑞→𝑝\n\nTrue in all cases →Tautology\nA contradiction is a compound proposition that is false for all possible truth values of its\npropositional variables.\nExample                                                                   p∧¬p\n2. CONTRADICTION\np ∧¬p\n¬p\np\nF\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nResult\np∧q\nq\np\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nT\nF\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\nF\nF\n(p∧q) ∧¬p\nImpossible to be true →Contradiction\nExample 3\n¬(𝑝∨¬𝑝)\n\nNegation of a tautology →Contradiction\nAlways false →Contradiction\nA contingency is a compound proposition that is true for some truth values and false for\nothers.\nExample                                                                   p∧q\n3. CONTINGENCY\np ∧q\nq\np\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\nF\nResult\np→q\nq\np\nT\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\np→q\nSometimes true, sometimes false →Contingency\nExample 3\np\nq\nTrue when both have same truth values →Contingency\nTrue only when both are true →Contingency",
