    "p": 26,
    "t": "A Well-Formed Formula (WFF) is a logically valid expression that is constructed according\nto the precise formation rules of logic.\nIn simple words:\nA WFF is a formula that is syntactically correct, just like a grammatically correct sentence in a\nlanguage.\nLogic has its own grammar rules, and only expressions that follow these rules are considered\nmeaningful.\nAny logical expression that violates these rules is not a WFF, even if it “looks logical”.\nWELL-FORMED FORMULA (WFF)\nThe Good Man\nPassword\nRecipe\nMobile Number\nProgramming\n\"Language has grammar rules.\"\n\"Logic also has grammar rules.\"\n\"If we violate those rules, the meaning changes—or disappears.\"\nThat's exactly what a Well-Formed Formula (WFF) is.\n\"A Well-Formed Formula is simply a logical sentence written according to the grammar rules of\nlogic.\"\nWHY WFF?\nThe idea of WFF is crucial because logic is not just about meaning, but also about\nstructure.\nOnly WFFs can:\nHave truth values\nBe evaluated using truth tables\nBe used in proofs\nBe processed by computers\nNon-WFF expressions are invalid and cannot be analyzed logically.\nIn computer science, WFFs ensure:\nCorrect parsing of expressions\nValid program conditions\nProper interpretation by compilers and theorem provers\nIn propositional logic, a WFF is built from:\nPropositional variables (p, q, r, …)\nLogical connectives (¬, ∧, ∨, →,\n)\nParentheses for grouping\nA formula is well-formed only if it is constructed step-by-step using allowed rules.\nWHY THE CONCEPT OF WFF IS IMPORTANT\nFORMATION RULES OF WFF\nRule 1: Atomic Propositions\nAny propositional variable such\nas p, q, r is a WFF.\nExamples:\n•p\n•q\n•r\nRule 2: Negation Rule\nIf A is a WFF, then ¬A is also a WFF.\nExamples:\n•¬p\n•¬(p ∧q)\nRule 3: Binary Connective Rule\nIf A and B are WFFs, then the\nfollowing are also WFFs:\n•(A ∧B)\n•(A ∨B)\n•(A →B)\n•(A\nB)\nExamples:\n•(p ∧q)\n•(¬p →q)\n•((p ∧q) →r)\nRule 4: Closure Rule\nNothing is a WFF unless it can be obtained\nby repeated application of Rules 1–3.\nThis rule prevents illegal expressions from\nbeing accepted.",
