    "t": "Example 1\nSpecification: The printer prints only if it has paper and ink.\nLet:\np: Printer prints\nq: Printer has paper\nr: Printer has ink\nLogical expression:\n𝑝→𝑞∧𝑟\n------------------\nExample 2\nSpecification: If the database is corrupted, then the system\nenters recovery mode.\nLet:\np: Database is corrupted\nq: System enters recovery mode\nLogical expression:\n𝑝→𝑞\nExample 3\nSpecification:\nThe user is authenticated if and only if\nthe password is correct.\nLet:\n•p: User is authenticated\n•q: Password is correct\nLogical expression:\n𝑝\n𝑞\nLogical connectives are used extensively in searches of large collections of information, such as\nindexes of web pages.\nBecause these searches employ techniques from propositional logic, they are called Boolean\nsearches.\nIn Boolean searches:\nThe connective AND is used to match records that contain both search terms.\nThe connective OR is used to match records that contain one or both of the search terms.\nThe connective NOT (sometimes written as AND NOT) is used to exclude a particular search term.\n3. BOOLEAN SEARCHES\nMost web search engines support Boolean searching techniques, which help find web pages\nabout particular subjects.\nFor instance, using Boolean searching to find web pages about universities in New Mexico:\nTo look for pages matching NEW AND MEXICO AND UNIVERSITIES\nThis retrieves pages containing all three words.\nTo find pages that deal with universities in New Mexico or Arizona\nSearch for: 𝑁𝐸𝑊  𝐴𝑁𝐷  𝑀𝐸𝑋𝐼𝐶𝑂  𝑂𝑅  𝐴𝑅𝐼𝑍𝑂𝑁𝐴  𝐴𝑁𝐷  𝑈𝑁𝐼𝑉𝐸𝑅𝑆𝐼𝑇𝐼𝐸𝑆\nTo find pages about universities in Mexico but not New Mexico\nSearch for: 𝑀𝐸𝑋𝐼𝐶𝑂  𝐴𝑁𝐷  𝑈𝑁𝐼𝑉𝐸𝑅𝑆𝐼𝑇𝐼𝐸𝑆  𝑁𝑂𝑇  𝑁𝐸𝑊\n(NOT may be replaced by a minus sign “–” in many search engines)\nEXAMPLE: WEB PAGE SEARCHING\nExample 1\nSearch: Find articles about Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning.\nSearch expression: AI AND MACHINE LEARNING\nExample 2\nSearch: Find documents related to networks or security.\nSearch expression: NETWORKS OR SECURITY\nExample 3\nSearch: Find pages about Java but not JavaScript.\nSearch expression: JAVA NOT JAVASCRIPT",
