    "p": 31,
    "t": "Puzzles that can be solved using logical reasoning are known as logic puzzles. Solving logic puzzles\nis an excellent way to practice working with the rules of logic.\nComputer programs designed to carry out logical reasoning often use well-known logic puzzles to\nillustrate their capabilities.\nExample (Raymond Smullyan)\nAn island has two kinds of inhabitants:\nKnights, who always tell the truth\nKnaves, who always lie\nYou go to the island and meet A and B.\nA says: “B is a knight.”\nB says: “The two of us are of opposite types.”\nQuestion:What are the types of A and B?\n4. LOGIC PUZZLES\nExample (Raymond Smullyan)\nAn island has two kinds of inhabitants:\nKnights, who always tell the truth\nKnaves, who always lie\nYou go to the island and meet A and B.\nA says: “B is a knight.”\nB says: “The two of us are of opposite\ntypes.”\nQuestion:What are the types of A and\nB?\nSolution\nLet:\n•p : A is a knight   and     ¬p : A is a knave\n•q : B is a knight   and     ¬q : B is a knave\nAnalysis:\n•\nIf A is a knight, then B must be a knight (since knights tell the truth).\nThen p ∧q would be true.\nBut B says they are of opposite types, which would be false.\nHence, A cannot be a knight.\n•\nTherefore, A is a knave (¬p is true).\nSince knaves always lie, both A’s statement and B’s statement must be false.\nSo, B is also a knave (¬q is true).\nConclusion:\nA and B are both knaves\nA says: “B is a knave.”\nB says: “A is a knave.”\nAnalysis\nIf p is true →A tells truth →B is a knave →q is false\nThen B (a knave) lies →“A is a knave” is false →A is a knight\nIf p is false →A lies →B is a knight →q is true\nThen B tells truth →“A is a knave” is true\nConclusion\nTwo solutions: one is a knight and the other is a knave\n•p : A is a knight   and     ¬p : A is a knave\n•q : B is a knight   and     ¬q : B is a knave\nA says: “At least one of us is a knave.”\nStatement means: ¬𝑝∨¬𝑞\nAnalysis\nIf A is a knight →statement is true →at least one is\na knave\nSo B must be a knave\nIf A is a knave →statement is false →both must be\nknights (contradiction)\nConclusion\n𝐴 is a knight, 𝐵 is a knave\n3- people puzzle Statements:\nA says: “B and C are knaves.”\nB says: “A and C are of opposite types.”\nStep 1: Define propositional variables\np : A is a knight and ¬p : A is a knave\nq : B is a knight and ¬q : B is a knave\nr : C is a knight and ¬r : C is a knave\nStep 2: Translate\nA’s statement : “B and C are knaves” −    ¬𝑞∧¬𝑟\nB’s statement: “A and C are of opposite types”\nexactly one is a knight - This is XOR: 𝑝⊕𝑟\nCase 1: Assume A is a knight (p = T)\nThen A tells the truth:\n¬𝑞∧¬𝑟 is TRUE ⇒𝑞= 𝐹, 𝑟= 𝐹\nSo:\n•A = knight /////  B = knave //// C = knave\nNow check B’s statement:\n𝑝⊕𝑟= 𝑇⊕𝐹= 𝑇\nBut B is a knave, so B’s statement must be false\nContradiction\nCase 1 is impossible.\nCase 2: Assume A is a knave (p = F)\nThen A lies, so A’s statement is false:\n¬𝑞∧¬𝑟= 𝐹\nThat means at least one of q or r is true.\nNow check possibilities.",
