    "t": "Case 2a: q = T, r = T\nCheck B’s statement:\n𝑝⊕𝑟= 𝐹⊕𝑇= 𝑇\nB is a knight → statement true\nAll conditions satisfied.\nSo:\nA = knave\nB = knight\nC = knight\n Consistent\nCase 2b: q = T, r = F\nB’s statement:\n𝑝⊕𝑟= 𝐹⊕𝐹= 𝐹\nBut B is a knight →must tell\ntruth\nInconsistent.\nCase 2c: q = F, r = T\nB’s statement:\n𝑝⊕𝑟= 𝐹⊕𝑇= 𝑇\nBut B is a knave →must lie\nInconsistent.\nFinal Correct Conclusion\nThe only consistent assignment is:\n𝐴 is a knave,  𝐵 is a knight,   𝐶 is a knight\nStatements\n•A says: “B is a knight and C is a knave.”\n•B says: “A is a knave.”\n•C says nothing.\nLet\n•p: A is a knight\n•q: B is a knight\n•r: C is a knight\nLogical form\n•A: 𝑞∧¬𝑟\n•B: ¬𝑝\n•¬p : A is a knave\n•¬q : B is a knave\n•¬r : C is a knave\nCase 1: Assume A is a knight (p = T)\nThen A tells the truth, so A’s statement must be true:\n𝑞∧¬𝑟= 𝑇\nThis implies:\n•𝑞= 𝑇→B is a knight\n•𝑟= 𝐹→C is a knave\nNow check B’s statement:\n•B is a knight (q = T), so B must tell the truth\n•B says: ¬𝑝\nBut:\n•𝑝= 𝑇⇒¬𝑝= 𝐹\nContradiction\nA knight (B) cannot make a false statement.\nCase 1 is impossible\nCase 2: Assume A is a knave (p = F)\nThen A lies, so A’s statement must be false:\n𝑞∧¬𝑟= 𝐹\nThis means:\n•Either 𝑞= 𝐹\nor\n𝑟= 𝑇(or both)\nNow check B’s statement:\n•B says: ¬𝑝\n•Since 𝑝= 𝐹, we get ¬𝑝= 𝑇\nSo B’s statement is true, which means:\n•B must be a knight →𝑞= 𝑇\nNow go back to A’s statement being false:\n•𝑞∧¬𝑟= 𝐹\n•Since 𝑞= 𝑇, we must have:\n•\n¬𝑟= 𝐹⇒𝑟= 𝑇\nConclusion\n𝐴 is a knave,   𝐵 is a knight,  𝐶 is a knight\nNow verify everything:\nA is a knave →lies →statement false\nB is a knight →tells truth\nC makes no statement →no issue\nStatements   -\nneed two teams\n•A says: “At least one of B or C is a knave.” - B says: “C is a knight.” - C says: “A is a knave.”\nLogical form                                    A: ¬𝑞∨¬𝑟\nB: 𝑟\nC: ¬𝑝\n Case 1: Assume A is a knight (p = T)\nThen A tells the truth, so A’s statement must be true:\n¬𝑞∨¬𝑟= 𝑇\nSo at least one of 𝑞, 𝑟is false.\nNow check C’s statement:\nC says ¬𝑝\n•But 𝑝= 𝑇⇒¬𝑝= 𝐹\nSo C’s statement is false, meaning:\n•C is a knave → 𝑟= 𝐹\nNow check B’s statement:\nB says 𝑟\n•Since 𝑟= 𝐹, B’s statement is false\nSo B is a knave →𝑞= 𝐹\nNow verify A’s statement:\n¬𝑞∨¬𝑟= 𝑇∨𝑇= 𝑇\nEverything is consistent.\nA knight\nB knave\nC knave\nFinal Conclusion\n(1) A knight, B knave, C knave\nis this\nfinal ??????\nCase 2: Assume A is a knave (p = F)\nThen A lies, so A’s statement must be false:\n¬𝑞∨¬𝑟= 𝐹\nThis implies:       𝑞= 𝑇  and  𝑟= 𝑇\nSo:   B is a knight      -\nC is a knight\nNow check B’s statement:    B says 𝑟\nSince 𝑟= 𝑇, statement is true\nFinal Conclusion\nThere are TWO consistent solutions:\n(1) A knight, B knave, C knave\n(2) A knave, B knight, C knight\nStatements\n•A says: “At least one of B or C is a knave.” - B says: “C is a knight.” - C says: “A is a knave.”\nLogical form                                    A: ¬𝑞∨¬𝑟\nB: 𝑟\nC: ¬𝑝\nCheck C’s statement:\n C says ¬𝑝\n Since 𝑝= 𝐹⇒¬𝑝= 𝑇\nC is a knight and tells the truth\nNow check A’s statement:\n•A is a knave and must lie\n ¬𝑞∨¬𝑟= 𝐹∨𝐹= 𝐹\nA’s statement is false\nEverything is consistent.\n A knave          -           B knight          C knight",
