    "t": "Statements\n•A says: “B and C are of the same type.”            B says: “A is a knight.”          C says: “B is a knave.”\nLogical form       A: 𝑞\n𝑟             B: 𝑝              C: ¬𝑞\nCase 1: Assume A is a knight (p = T)\nThen A tells the truth, so:\n𝑞\n𝑟= 𝑇\nThis means:\neither 𝑞= 𝑇, 𝑟= 𝑇\nor 𝑞= 𝐹, 𝑟= 𝐹\nNow check B’s statement:\nB says 𝑝\nSince 𝑝= 𝑇, B’s statement is true\nTherefore B is a knight →𝑞= 𝑇\nNow return to A’s condition:\n•𝑞= 𝑇, so to satisfy 𝑞\n𝑟,\n•we must have 𝑟= 𝑇\nSo far:     A knight   - B knight    -\nC knight\nNow check C’s statement:\nC says ¬𝑞\nBut 𝑞= 𝑇⇒¬𝑞= 𝐹\nC is a knight, but made a false statement\nContradiction\nCase 1 rejected.\nStatements\n•A says: “B and C are of the same type.”            B says: “A is a knight.”          C says: “B is a knave.”\nLogical form       A: 𝑞\n𝑟                B: 𝑝               C: ¬𝑞\nCase 2: Assume A is a knave (p = F)\nThen A lies, so:\n𝑞\n𝑟= 𝐹\nThis means:\n𝑞≠𝑟(one knight, one knave)\nNow check B’s statement:\nB says 𝑝\n•Since 𝑝= 𝐹, B’s statement is false\n•Therefore B is a knave →𝑞= 𝐹\nNow return to A’s condition:\n𝑞\n𝑟= 𝐹\n•Since 𝑞= 𝐹, this forces:      𝑟= 𝑇\nSo:\n•A knave      B knave        C knight\nNow check C’s statement:\nC says ¬𝑞\nSince 𝑞= 𝐹, ¬𝑞= 𝑇\nC is a knight →statement true\nNow verify A’s statement:\n𝑞\n𝑟= 𝐹\n𝑇= 𝐹\nA is a knave →lies\nEverything is consistent.\nCorrect Final Conclusion\n𝐴 is a knave,  𝐵 is a knave,  𝐶 is a knight\nStatements\n•A says: “Exactly one of us is a knight.”  - B says: “C is a knave.”    -\nC says: “A is a knave.”\nLogical form         A: 𝑝⊕𝑞⊕𝑟   or\n𝑝∧¬𝑞∧¬𝑟   ∨   ¬𝑝∧𝑞∧¬𝑟   ∨   ¬𝑝∧¬𝑞∧𝑟\nB: ¬𝑟               C: ¬𝑝\nCase 1: Assume A is a knight (p = T)\nThen A tells the truth, so A’s statement must be\ntrue.\nSo exactly one knight exists.\nSince A is already a knight:\n𝑞= 𝐹,   𝑟= 𝐹\nNow check B:\nB says ¬𝑟\nSince 𝑟= 𝐹, ¬𝑟= 𝑇\nSo B tells the truth →B is a knight\nContradiction   (B cannot be both knight and\nknave.)\nReject Case 1\nStatements\n•A says: “Exactly one of us is a knight.”  - B says: “C is a knave.”    -\nC says: “A is a knave.”\nLogical form         A: 𝑝⊕𝑞⊕𝑟   or\n𝑝∧¬𝑞∧¬𝑟   ∨   ¬𝑝∧𝑞∧¬𝑟   ∨   ¬𝑝∧¬𝑞∧𝑟\nB: ¬𝑟               C: ¬𝑝\nCase 2: Assume A is a knave (p = F)\nThen A lies, so A’s statement is false.\nThat means:\nIt is NOT true that exactly one of A, B, C is a knight\n→Either zero (0) , two (2) , or three (3) knights.\nNow check C:\nC says ¬𝑝\nSince 𝑝= 𝐹, ¬𝑝= 𝑇\nSo C tells the truth →C is a knight        Hence 𝑟= 𝑇\nNow check B:     B says ¬𝑟\nBut 𝑟= 𝑇⇒¬𝑟= 𝐹\nSo B lies →B is a knave        Hence 𝑞= 𝐹\nNow count knights:\nA = knave           B = knave                C = knight\nExactly one knight\nBut A said “Exactly one of us is a knight”\nand A is a knave, so his statement must be false.\nContradiction\n(A’s statement turned out true, but A is a knave.)\nReject Case 2\nFinal Result\nNO consistent solution exists\nThis set of statements is logically impossible.",
