    "t": "PUZZLE : Who Broke the Window\nStatements                  A: “B broke it.”                  B: “C broke it.”                   C: “I did not break it.”\nExactly one statement is false\nStep 1: Let\n•p: A broke it\n•q: B broke it\n•r: C broke it\nExactly one person broke it.\nStep 2: Translate\n•A: q\n•B: r\n•C: ¬r\nStep 3: Test\nIf C broke it (r = T):\n•A: False\n•B: True\n•C: False\n(2 false)\nIf B broke it (q = T):\n•A: True\n•B: False\n•C: True\n(only one false)\nConclusion\n𝐵 broke the window\nPUZZLE : Project Submission\nStatements   A: “B and C submitted.”                 B: “Only one of us submitted.”               C: “A did not submit.”\nExactly one statement is true\nLet                                           p: A submitted         q: B submitted        r: C submitted\nTranslate\n•A: 𝑞∧𝑟\n•B: exactly one of p, q, r\n•C: ¬𝑝\nCase 1: A alone submitted\n𝑝= 𝑇,   𝑞= 𝐹,   𝑟= 𝐹\nEvaluate statements:\nA: 𝑞∧𝑟= 𝐹→false\nB: “only one submitted” →true\nC: ¬𝑝= 𝐹→false\nExactly one statement true\nThis case satisfies all conditions\nCase 2: B alone submitted\n𝑝= 𝐹,   𝑞= 𝑇,   𝑟= 𝐹\nEvaluate:\nA: 𝑞∧𝑟= 𝐹→false\nB: “only one submitted” →true\nC: ¬𝑝= 𝑇→true\nTwo statements true →reject\nCase 3: C alone submitted\n𝑝= 𝐹,   𝑞= 𝐹,   𝑟= 𝑇\nEvaluate:\n•A: 𝑞∧𝑟= 𝐹→ false\n•B: “only one submitted” → true\n•C: ¬𝑝= 𝑇→ true\n Two statements true → reject\nPUZZLE : Project Submission\nStatements   A: “B and C submitted.”                 B: “Only one of us submitted.”               C: “A did not submit.”\nExactly one statement is true\nLet                                           p: A submitted         q: B submitted        r: C submitted\nCase 4: A and B submitted\n𝑝= 𝑇,   𝑞= 𝑇,   𝑟= 𝐹\nEvaluate:\n•A: 𝑞∧𝑟= 𝐹→false\n•B: “only one submitted” →false\n•C: ¬𝑝= 𝐹→false\nZero true →reject\nCase 5: A and C submitted\n𝑝= 𝑇,   𝑞= 𝐹,   𝑟= 𝑇\nEvaluate:\nA: 𝑞∧𝑟= 𝐹→false\nB: “only one submitted” →false\nC: ¬𝑝= 𝐹→false\nZero true →reject\nCase 6: B and C submitted\n𝑝= 𝐹,   𝑞= 𝑇,   𝑟= 𝑇\nEvaluate:\n•A: 𝑞∧𝑟= 𝑇→true\n•B: “only one submitted” →false\n•C: ¬𝑝= 𝑇→true\nTwo true →reject\nCase 7: All three submitted\n𝑝= 𝑇,   𝑞= 𝑇,   𝑟= 𝑇\nEvaluate:\n•A: 𝑞∧𝑟= 𝑇→true\n•B: “only one submitted” →false\n•C: ¬𝑝= 𝐹→false\nExactly one statement true\nThis case satisfies all conditions\n PUZZLE : Project Submission\nStatements   A: “B and C submitted.”                 B: “Only one of us submitted.”               C: “A did not submit.”\n Exactly one statement is true\nLet                                           p: A submitted         q: B submitted        r: C submitted\nCase 8: None submitted\n𝑝= 𝐹,   𝑞= 𝐹,   𝑟= 𝐹\nEvaluate:\n•A: 𝑞∧𝑟= 𝐹→false\n•B: “only one submitted” →false\n•C: ¬𝑝= 𝑇→true\nExactly one statement true\nThis case also satisfies all conditions\nFinal Correct Conclusion\nThere are THREE valid solutions:\n\n(𝟏) Only A Submitted\n\n(𝟐) All three Submitted\n\n(𝟑) None Submitted\nVery Important Rule\nWhen puzzles say:\n“Exactly one statement is true”\nAlways:\n1.Enumerate all possible cases\n2.Evaluate every statement\n3.Count true statements\n4.Accept all cases that satisfy the condition\nNever stop after finding one working case.",
