    "t": "Three students A, B, and C attended an exam. Each makes a statement:\n•A: “B attended the exam.”\n•B: “C did not attend the exam.”\n•C: “A and B attended the exam.”\nExactly two of these statements are true.\nLet\n•p: A attended\n•q: B attended\n•r: C attended\n Final Correct Conclusion\nThere are TWO valid solutions:\n𝐴𝑙𝑙 𝑡ℎ𝑟𝑒𝑒 𝑎𝑡𝑡𝑒𝑛𝑑𝑒𝑑\n𝑂𝑛𝑙𝑦 𝐵 𝐴𝑡𝑡𝑒𝑛𝑑𝑒𝑑\nPUZZLE : Who Is Online\nStatements\n•A: “B is online.”\n•B: “C is offline.”\n•C: “A is offline.”\n•Exactly two users online and two statements true\nLet  p: A online    /     q: B online    /       r: C online\nTranslate statements into logic\nA’s statement:    “B is online” →𝑞\nB’s statement:    “C is offline” →¬𝑟\nC’s statement:    “A is offline” →¬𝑝\nEnumerate cases with exactly TWO users online\nThere are only three possible cases.\nCase 1: A and B online:     𝑝= 𝑇,   𝑞= 𝑇,   𝑟= 𝐹- A: 𝑞=𝑇→true |  B: ¬𝑟=𝑇→true  |  C: ¬𝑝=𝐹→false\nExactly two statements true   -\nExactly two users online   -\nValid\nCase 2: A and C online:     𝑝= 𝑇,   𝑞= 𝐹,   𝑟= 𝑇\n- A: 𝑞=𝐹→false   |  B: ¬𝑟=𝐹→false  |  C: ¬𝑝=𝐹→false\nZero true →reject\nCase 3: B and C online:     𝑝= 𝐹,   𝑞= 𝑇,   𝑟= 𝑇- A: 𝑞=𝑇→true  |    B: ¬𝑟=𝐹→false   |  C: ¬𝑝=𝑇→true\nExactly two statements true   -\nExactly two users online   -\nValid\nPUZZLE : Department Meeting\nStatements\n•A: “If B attended, then C attended.”\n•B: “C did not attend.”\n•C: “A attended.”\n•Exactly two statements true\nLet\n•p: A attended\n•q: B attended\n•r: C attended\nFinal Correct Conclusion\nThere are FOUR valid solutions:\n (1) A, B, C all attended\n(2) A and B attended\n(3) A and C attended\n(4) None attended\n# True\nC: 𝒑\nB: ¬𝒓\nA: 𝒒→𝒓\nr\nq\np\nCase\nT\nF\nT\nT\nT\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nGiven\nEach student may or may not own a laptop.\nStatements             A: “B owns a laptop.”         -\nB: “C owns a laptop.”    -\nC: “A does not own a laptop.”\nCondition\n Exactly ONE statement is true.\nStep 1: Define propositional variables\nLet:         p : A owns a laptop             q : B owns a laptop            r : C owns a laptop\nStep 2: Translate statements into logic\n•A’s statement:      “B owns a laptop” →𝑞\n•B’s statement:      “C owns a laptop” →𝑟\n•C’s statement:      “A does not own a laptop” →¬𝑝\n Final Correct Conclusion\nThere are THREE valid solutions:\nA and B Owns Laptop\nA and C owns Laptop\nNone owns a Laptop\nValid?\n# True\nC: ¬p\nB: r\nA: q\nr\nq\np\nCase\nF\nT\nT\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nT\nF\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\nF\nF\nT\nT\nT\nT\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nF\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nT\nF\nF\nF\nF\nF",
