    "p": 36,
    "t": "Computers represent information using bits.\nA bit is a symbol with two possible values:\n0 (zero)\n1 (one)\nA bit represents a binary digit. Zeros and ones are the digits used in binary representation of\nnumbers.\nA bit can also be used to represent a truth value, because there are exactly two truth values:\nTrue (T) →1\nFalse (F) →0\nA variable is called a Boolean variable if its value is either true or false. A Boolean variable\ncan be represented using a bit.\n5. LOGIC AND BIT OPERATIONS\nBit Operations and Logical Connectives\nComputer bit operations correspond to logical connectives.\nIn truth tables:\nTrue is replaced by 1\nFalse is replaced by 0\nTruth Table for Bit Operations\nBit Strings\nA bit string is a sequence of zero or more bits.\nThe length of a string is the number of bits in the string.\nExample:\n101010011\nis a bit string of length 9.\nBit Operation\nLogical Operation\nOR\nOR\nAND\nAND\n⊕\nXOR\nx XOR y\nx AND y\nx OR y\ny\nx\nBitwise OR\n0110110110\n1100011101\n----------------\n1110111111\nEXAMPLES: FIND THE BITWISE OR, BITWISE AND, AND BITWISE XOR OF THE BIT STRINGS:\nBitwise AND\n0110110110\n1100011101\n----------------\n0100010100\nBitwise XOR\n0110110110\n1100011101\n----------------\n1010101011\n1.\nNOT Operation (Bitwise Complement) - Negation (¬p)\n2.\nNAND Operation (NOT AND) - 𝑝↑𝑞= ¬ 𝑝∧𝑞\n3.\nNOR Operation (NOT OR) - 𝑝↓𝑞= ¬ 𝑝∨𝑞\n4.\nXNOR Operation (Equivalence) - Biconditional − 𝑝\n𝑞\n5.\nImplication as a Bit Operation -Logical Connective - 𝑝→𝑞\nEquivalent Logical Form - ¬𝑝∨𝑞\n6.\nBitwise Shift Operations\nLeft Shift (<<) - 1011 << 1 = 10110\nRight Shift (>>) - 1011 >> 1 = 0101\n7.\nBit Masking (AND + NOT)\nBit masking is a technique used in computer systems to select, clear, or modify specific bits in a\nbinary number while leaving the other bits unchanged.\nA mask is a binary pattern that determines which bits are affected during an operation.\nOTHER BIT OPERATIONS AND LOGICAL CONNECTIVES",
