    "p": 37,
    "t": "Example\nData   =           1 1 0 1 0 1\nMask  =          0 0 1 1 0 0\nStep 1: Apply NOT to the mask\n¬Mask =        1 1 0 0 1 1\nStep 2: AND Data with ¬Mask\n1 1 0 1 0 1   (Data)\nAND                    1 1 0 0 1 1   (¬Mask)\n--------------\n1 1 0 0 0 1   (Result)\nExplanation\nBits corresponding to 1s in the original mask are cleared\nOther bits remain unchanged\nTruth Table View (AND + NOT)\np ∧¬q\n¬q\nMask\nbit (q)\nData\nbit (p)\nEx 1: Clearing Middle Bits\nData   = 1 0 1 1 0 1 1 0\nMask  = 0 0 1 1 1 1 0 0\nEx 2: Clearing Least Significant Bits\nData    = 1 1 0 1 1 0 0 1\nMask   = 0 0 0 0 0 1 1 1\nEx 3: Clearing Most Significant Bits\nData     = 1 1 1 0 1 0 0 1\nMask    = 1 1 1 0 0 0 0 0\nEx 4: Selective Clearing (Alternating Bits)\nData      = 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0\nMask     = 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 1\nPropositional equivalences are pairs (or sets) of propositional logic expressions that have\nthe same truth value for every possible assignment of truth values to their propositional\nvariables.\nIn simple words:\nTwo propositions are propositionally equivalent if they are true and false in exactly the same\nsituations.\nFormal Definition\nLet P and Q be compound propositions.\nP and Q are said to be logically (propositionally) equivalent if:\n𝑃\n𝑄is a tautology.\nEven though P and Q may look different, they represent the same logical meaning.\nPROPOSITIONAL EQUIVALENCES\nExample 1: Implication Equivalence\n𝑝→𝑞   ≡  ¬𝑝∨𝑞\nBoth expressions produce identical truth tables, so they are equivalent.\nExample 2: Double Negation\n¬ ¬𝑝≡𝑝\nNegating a proposition twice gives back the original proposition.",
