    "p": 39,
    "t": "TRUTH TABLE METHOD FOR DECISION PROBLEMS\nThe most direct method for solving a decision problem in propositional logic is the\nconstruction of a truth table.\nIf a formula contains n propositional variables, then the truth table contains exactly 2ⁿ rows,\ncorresponding to all possible combinations of truth values.\nEach row represents a unique assignment of truth values, ensuring that all possibilities are\nexamined.\nDECIDABILITY OF STATEMENT CALCULUS\nSince truth tables always terminate after a finite number of steps, the decision problem in\nstatement calculus is decidable.\nThis means there exists an algorithm that determines whether any given propositional\nformula is a tautology, contradiction, or satisfiable.\nLimitations..?\nLIMITATIONS OF TRUTH TABLES\nAlthough truth tables provide a complete and systematic procedure solution method, they\nbecome impractical for formulas involving a large number of variables.\nEven with the aid of computers, truth tables can become excessively large.\nTherefore, alternative procedures are required.\nNEED FOR NORMAL FORMS\nBecause of the limitations of truth tables, alternative procedures are required.\nOne such approach is the reduction of formulas into standard representations known as normal\nforms, which simplify logical analysis.\nA normal form is a standardized way of writing a propositional formula so that its logical\nstructure becomes clear and systematic.\nNormal forms are extremely useful in logical analysis, automated reasoning, digital circuit\ndesign, and satisfiability testing.\nThe two most important normal forms are Disjunctive Normal Form (DNF) and Conjunctive\nNormal Form (CNF).\nPrincipal Forms are specific types of normal forms that are derived under certain conditions\nand constraints.\nNORMAL FORMS",
