    "p": 40,
    "t": "Normal Forms\nDisjunctive Normal Form (DNF)\nConjunctive Normal Form (CNF)\nPrincipal Normal Forms\nPrincipal Disjunctive Normal Form (PDNF)\nPrincipal Conjunctive Normal Form  (PCNF)\nTYPES OF NORMAL FORMS\nA compound proposition is said to be in Disjunctive Normal Form (DNF) if it is expressed as a\ndisjunction (OR) of one or more conjunctions (AND) of literals.\nA literal is either a propositional variable or its negation.\nFormally, given a compound proposition u, if we obtain an equivalent proposition v such that u ≡ v and v\nis a disjunction of compound propositions, each of which is a conjunction involving the variables of u or\ntheir negations, then v is called a Disjunctive Normal Form of u.\n“A formula which is equivalent to a given formula and which consists of a sum of elementary products is called a\ndisjunctive normal form of the given formula.”\nExample                                                 (P ∧~ Q) ∨(Q ∧R) ∨(~ P ∧Q ∧~ R)\n(P∧Q)∨(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧¬Q∧¬R)\nDISJUNCTIVE NORMAL FORM (DNF)\nA product of propositional variables and/or\ntheir negations is called an elementary\nproduct.\nFor example, if p and q are propositional\nvariables, then\np,\n¬p,\np ∧ q,\np ∧ ¬q,\n¬p ∧ q, and\n¬p ∧ ¬q are elementary products.\nELEMENTARY PRODUCT AND ELEMENTARY SUM\n• A sum of propositional variables and/or their\nnegations is called an elementary sum.\n• Examples include\n•\np,\n•\n¬p ∨ q,\n•\np ∨ q, and\n•\n¬p ∨ q ∨ r.\n• An alternative definition of DNF states that a\nformula which is equivalent to a given formula\nand consists of a sum of elementary\nproducts is called the Disjunctive Normal\nForm of that formula.\nPROCEDURE TO OBTAIN DNF\n1.\nAn equivalent formula can be obtained by replacing →and\nwith ^, V and ┐.\nThe implication p →q is logically equivalent to ¬p ∨q. This equivalence is used to remove implication\noperators when converting formulas into normal forms.\nThe biconditional p\nq is equivalent to (p →q) ∧(q →p). After eliminating implications, only ∧, ∨, and\n¬ remain.\n2. Apply negation to the formula or to a part of the formula and not to the variables.\n3. Using DeMorgan's law, apply negation to variables.\n4. Repeated application of distributive laws will give the required DNF.\nExample 1: Obtain disjunctive normal forms of P ^ (P →Q)\"\nSolution Let S\nP^(P→Q)\nP ^ (┐PVQ) [ P→Q\n┐PVQ]\n(P^┐P) V (PɅQ)",
