    "p": 41,
    "t": "Example: Convert (A ∨B) ∧(C ∨D) to DNF: (A ∧¬B) ∨(C ∧D)\n1. The expression doesn't contain implications or bi-conditionals.\n2. No negations in the expression.\n3. (A ∨B) ∧(C ∨D) = [(A ∨B) ∧C] ∨[(A ∨B) ∧D]\n= [(A ∧C) ∨(B ∧C)] ∨[(A ∧D) ∨(B ∧D) ]\n= (A ∧C) ∨(B ∧C) ∨(A ∧D) ∨(B ∧D)\nExample: Convert the expression (A ∨B) ∧(¬A ∨C) to Disjunctive Normal Form (DNF)\nSolution:\nDistribute the AND over OR:\n(A ∨B) ∧(¬A ∨C) = (A ∧¬A) ∨(A ∧C) ∨(B ∧¬A) ∨(B ∧C)\nSimplify:\n(A ∧C) ∨(B ∧¬A) ∨(B ∧C)\nExample 1\nConvert to DNF: 𝑝→𝑞∧𝑟\nSolution     𝑝→𝑞∧𝑟≡¬𝑝∨𝑞∧𝑟\nThis is already in DNF:\n¬𝑝   ∨   𝑞∧𝑟\nExample 2\nConvert to DNF:\n𝑝∨𝑞∧𝑟\nSolution\nApply distributive law:\n𝑝∧𝑟   ∨   𝑞∧𝑟\nExample 3\nConvert to DNF:\n¬ 𝑝→𝑞\nSolution\n¬ 𝑝→𝑞≡¬ ¬𝑝∨𝑞≡𝑝∧¬𝑞\n𝑝∧¬𝑞\nExample 4\nConvert to DNF:\n𝑝∧𝑞∨𝑝∧¬𝑞\nSolution\nFactor 𝑝:\n𝑝∧𝑞∨¬𝑞\nSince 𝑞∨¬𝑞= 𝑇,\n𝑝\nExample 5\nConvert to DNF:\n𝑝∨𝑞∧¬𝑝∨𝑟\nSolution\nApply distributive law:\n𝑝∧¬𝑝   ∨   𝑝∧𝑟   ∨   𝑞∧¬𝑝   ∨   𝑞∧𝑟\nEliminate contradiction 𝑝∧¬𝑝:\n𝑝∧𝑟   ∨   𝑞∧¬𝑝   ∨   𝑞∧𝑟\nObtain the Disjunctive Normal Form of: 𝑞   ∨   𝑝∧𝑟   ∧  ¬\n𝑝∨𝑟∧𝑞\nStep 1: By operator precedence ( ¬ > ∧> ∨), the correct interpretation is:\n𝒒   ∨  [ 𝒑∧𝒓∧¬\n𝒑∨𝒓∧𝒒]\nStep 2: Apply De Morgan’s Law                  ¬\n𝑝∨𝑟∧𝑞   ≡  ¬ 𝑝∨𝑟   ∨  ¬𝑞\n𝑞   ∨   𝑝∧𝑟   ∧   ¬ 𝑝∨𝑟   ∨  ¬𝑞\nStep 3: Apply De Morgan’s Law again                               ¬ 𝑝∨𝑟   ≡  ¬𝑝∧¬𝑟\n𝑞   ∨   𝑝∧𝑟   ∧\n¬𝑝∧¬𝑟   ∨  ¬𝑞\nStep 4: Apply the extended distributive law\nDistribute 𝑝∧𝑟over the disjunction:                         𝑞   ∨   𝑝∧𝑟∧¬𝑝∧¬𝑟   ∨   𝑝∧𝑟∧¬𝑞\nStep 5: Apply the negation (contradiction) laws\n𝑝∧¬𝑝= 𝐹, 𝑟∧¬𝑟= 𝐹\nThus: 𝑞   ∨   𝑝∧𝑟∧¬𝑞\n\nFinal Answer (DNF) 𝑞   ∨   𝑝∧¬𝑞∧𝑟\nThis is the required Disjunctive Normal Form, since it is a disjunction of conjunctions of literals.\nEX",
