    "t": "4. 𝑃→𝑄∧𝑅\n∧\n¬𝑃→¬𝑄∧¬𝑅\nStep 1: Eliminate implications = ¬𝑃∨𝑄∧𝑅\n∧𝑃∨¬𝑄∧¬𝑅\nStep 2: Rearrange =\n¬𝑃∨𝑄∧𝑅\n∧¬𝑅∧𝑃∨¬𝑄\nStep 3: Distribute =\n¬𝑃∧¬𝑅∨𝑄∧𝑅∧¬𝑅\n∧𝑃∨¬𝑄\nStep 4: Complement 𝑅∧¬𝑅= F\n= ¬𝑃∧¬𝑅∧𝑃∨¬𝑄\nStep 5: Distribute : = ¬𝑃∧¬𝑅∧𝑃∨¬𝑃∧¬𝑅∧¬𝑄\nStep 6: Complement: = F ∨¬𝑃∧¬𝑄∧¬𝑅\nStep 7: Identity: ¬𝑃∧¬𝑄∧¬𝑅\n5. ¬𝑃∨¬𝑄→¬𝑃∧𝑅\nStep 1: Eliminate implication: = ¬ ¬𝑃∨¬𝑄∨¬𝑃∧𝑅\nStep 2: De Morgan: = 𝑃∧𝑄∨¬𝑃∧𝑅\nAlready DNF. : 𝑃∧𝑄∨¬𝑃∧𝑅\n6. ¬𝑃→¬𝑄∨𝑃↓𝑄\nStep 1: Eliminate implication: = 𝑃∨¬𝑄∨𝑃↓𝑄\nStep 2: Replace NOR: = 𝑃∨¬𝑄∨¬ 𝑃∨𝑄\nStep 3: De Morgan: = 𝑃∨¬𝑄∨¬𝑃∧¬𝑄\nStep 4: Distribute OR over AND (to express fully in DNF)\nThis is already an OR of literals and a conjunction: 𝑃∨¬𝑄∨¬𝑃∧¬𝑄\nThis is a valid DNF since it is a disjunction of conjunctions (single literals are conjunctions of one\nliteral).\nIn propositional logic, a compound proposition is said to be in Conjunctive Normal Form\n(CNF) if it is expressed as a conjunction (AND) of one or more disjunctions (OR) of literals.\nA literal is either a propositional variable itself or the negation of a propositional variable.\nThus, a formula in CNF has the general structure:\ndisjunction of literals    ∧   disjunction of literals    ∧   ⋯\nFormally, a formula is said to be in Conjunctive Normal Form if it is logically equivalent to a\ngiven formula and consists of a product (conjunction) of elementary sums.\nAn elementary sum is a disjunction of literals. Each conjunct in a CNF expression is called a\nclause.\nIt is important to note that the Conjunctive Normal Form of a given formula is not unique.\nDifferent applications of distributive laws may lead to different CNF expressions, but all such\nexpressions are logically equivalent to the original formula.\nEx:                 p∧(¬p∨q)\n|             (¬p∨q)∧(¬p∨r)\nCONJUNCTIVE NORMAL FORM (CNF)\nTo convert a given propositional formula into CNF, we first eliminate all implication (→) and\nbiconditional (\n) connectives using their logical equivalents.\nAfter this step, the formula contains only the connectives ¬, ∨, and ∧.\nNext, De Morgan’s laws are applied to push negations inward so that negation applies only to\nindividual variables.\nFinally, the distributive laws are used to distribute disjunction over conjunction until the\nformula becomes a conjunction of disjunctions.\nPROCEDURE FOR CONVERTING A FORMULA INTO CNF",
