    "p": 44,
    "t": "Example 1\nConvert the following formula into CNF:\n𝑝∧𝑝→𝑞\nSolution\nFirst, eliminate the implication:\n𝑝∧𝑝→𝑞≡𝑝∧¬𝑝∨𝑞\nThe expression is already a conjunction of disjunctions of literals.\nHence, it is already in Conjunctive Normal Form.\n𝑝∧¬𝑝∨𝑞\nExample 2\nConvert the following formula into CNF:\n𝑝→𝑞∧𝑟\nSolution\nFirst, eliminate the implication:\n𝑝→𝑞∧𝑟≡¬𝑝∨𝑞∧𝑟\nNow apply the distributive law:\n¬𝑝∨𝑞∧𝑟≡¬𝑝∨𝑞∧¬𝑝∨𝑟\nThus, the Conjunctive Normal Form is:\n¬𝑝∨𝑞∧¬𝑝∨𝑟\nEX: Convert the following formula into CNF:\n𝑝∨𝑞∧¬𝑝∨𝑟\nSolution\nThis expression is already a conjunction of disjunctions of literals. Therefore, it is already\nin Conjunctive Normal Form.\n𝑝∨𝑞∧¬𝑝∨𝑟\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEx: Convert the following formula into CNF:\n¬ 𝑝∧𝑞\nSolution\nApply De Morgan’s law:\n¬ 𝑝∧𝑞≡¬𝑝∨¬𝑞\nThis is a single disjunction of literals and hence is in CNF.\n¬𝑝∨¬𝑞",
