    "t": "Imagine you are a detective solving a\nmystery.\nWhat do you do?\nYou collect evidence.\nFrom the evidence, you arrive at a conclusion.\nThis process of arriving at a logical\nconclusion from available information is\ncalled Inference.\nInference is simply reasoning.\nLEARNING RULES OF INFERENCE\nEveryday Example\nTeacher says:\nIf a student studies sincerely, then the student will score good marks.\nSuppose Ravi studies sincerely.\nCan we conclude Ravi will score good marks?\nYes.\nHow?\nBecause we inferred the conclusion from the given information.\nThat process is called Inference.\nEX:\nProofs in mathematics are valid arguments that establish the truth of mathematical statements.\nArgument\nAn argument in propositional logic is a sequence of propositions, or a sequence of statements that\nends with a conclusion.\nAll but the final proposition in the argument are called premises.\nThe final proposition is called the conclusion.\nAn argument is valid if the truth of all its premises implies that the conclusion is true.\nRULES OF INFERENCE\nFormal Definition\nInference is the logical process of deriving a new statement (called the conclusion) from one\nor more given statements (called premises) using valid logical rules.\nSimple Definition\nInference means drawing a logical conclusion from known facts.\nRULES OF INFERENCE…",
