    "p": 51,
    "t": "Why is Theory of Inference Important?\nComputers never guess.\nEvery decision made by a computer follows a logical rule.\nExamples:\nIF password is correct\nTHEN allow login\nIF balance < amount\nTHEN transaction failed\nIF packet received\nTHEN acknowledge\nIF sensor detects smoke\nTHEN alarm ON\nEvery one of these uses inference.\nValid Argument\nA valid argument is a sequence of propositions\n𝑃ଵ,𝑃ଶ, … , 𝑃௡called premises,\ntogether with a proposition C, called the conclusion, such that the implication\n𝑃ଵ∧𝑃ଶ∧⋯∧𝑃௡→𝐶is a tautology.\nPremises are the statements assumed to be true.\nThey provide the starting point.\nWithout premises, there is no reasoning.\nAn argument is valid if, whenever each of its premises 𝑷𝟏,𝑷𝟐, … , 𝑷𝒏is true, its conclusion 𝑪is also true.\nEach inference can be written as an implication:\nConjunction of premises →Conclusion\nThe conclusion is the logical consequence obtained from the premises.\nThe conclusion is valid if the implication is a tautology.\nArgument Form\nAn argument form in propositional logic is a sequence of compound propositions involving\npropositional variables.\nAn argument form is valid if, no matter which propositions are substituted for the propositional\nvariables, whenever all the premises are true, the conclusion is also true.\nPurpose of Rules of Inference\nRules of inference provide the justification of the steps used in a proof.\nInference is an idea or conclusion that is drawn from evidence and reasoning.\nExample\nAll birds have wings.\nParrot is a bird.\nConclusion\nParrot has wings.\nArgument Form\nPremise 1\nPremise 2\nPremise 3\n↓\nConclusion\nEvery inference has three parts.\n1. Premises\nKnown facts.\n2. Reasoning\nApplying logical rules.\n3. Conclusion\nNew fact obtained.\nSTRUCTURE OF INFERENCE",
