    "p": 55,
    "t": "Example : Programming\nLet\nP: The program compiles successfully.\nQ: The executable file is created.\nR: The program runs.\nPremises:\nIf the program compiles successfully, then the\nexecutable file is created.\nIf the executable file is created, then the\nprogram runs.\nConclusion:\nTherefore, if the program compiles\nsuccessfully, then the program runs.\nExample : College\nLet\n•\nP: I attend all classes.\n•\nQ: My attendance is above 75%.\n•\nR: I am eligible to write the exam.\nPremises:\n1. If I attend all classes, then my attendance is above\n75%.\n2. If my attendance is above 75%, then I am eligible to\nwrite the exam.\nConclusion:\nTherefore, if I attend all classes, then I am eligible\nto write the exam.\n𝒑→𝒒, 𝒒→𝒓⟹𝒑→𝒓\n𝒑→𝒒,   𝒒→𝒓   ∴𝒑→𝒓\nName\nTautology\nForm\nRule of Inference\nNo\nAddition\nAdding an extra option\nTautology\n𝑝→𝑝∨𝑞\n𝑝 ⟹𝑝∨𝑞\n𝑝     ∴𝑝∨𝑞\nConjunction\nJoining statements\nTautology\n𝑝∧𝑞\n→𝑝∧𝑞\n𝑝, 𝑞⟹𝑝∧𝑞\n𝑝,   𝑞     ∴𝑝∧𝑞\nSimplification\nExtracting part of a conjunction\nTautology\n𝑝∧𝑞→𝑝\n𝑝∧𝑞⟹𝑝\n𝑝∧𝑞     ∴𝑝\nTABLE 1: RULES OF INFERENCE FOR PROPOSITIONAL LOGIC…\nName\nTautolo\ngy\nForm\nRule of Inference\nNo\nResolution\nResolving (eliminating) a variable\nThe variable p appears positively in one clause\nThe same variable ¬p appears negatively in\nanother clause\nThey cancel out, leaving the remaining literals\nTautology\n𝑝∨𝑞∧¬𝑝∨𝑟\n→𝑞∨𝑟\n𝑝∨𝑞, ¬𝑝∨𝑟⟹𝑞∨𝑟\n𝑝∨𝑞,   ¬𝑝∨𝑟     ∴𝑞∨𝑟\nTABLE 1: RULES OF INFERENCE FOR PROPOSITIONAL LOGIC…\nCase 1: p is TRUE\nThen second clause forces:  𝑟\nSo r is true →𝑞∨𝑟is true.\nCase 2: p is FALSE\nThen first clause forces:  𝑞\nSo q is true →𝑞∨𝑟is true.\nIn both cases:    𝑞∨𝑟   must be true.\nThat is why resolution is logically valid.\nTo prove using resolution, we show:\nFrom the clauses\n1.  𝒑∨𝒒\n2.  ¬𝒑∨𝒓\nwe can derive:   𝒒∨𝒓\nThink logically:  If 𝒑∨𝒒 means: either p is true OR q is true.\nAnd ¬𝑝∨𝑟 means: either p is false OR r is true.\nNow consider two cases:\nRule P (Rule of Premise)\nDefinition\nA Rule P means that the statement is directly taken from the given premises. No inference is\nperformed.\nSymbol 𝐑𝐮𝐥𝐞 𝐏 = 𝐏𝐫𝐞𝐦𝐢𝐬𝐞\n-----------\nRule T (Theory Rule)\nDefinition\nA formula S may be introduced in a derivation if S is a tautology implied by one or more preceding\nformulas.\n-----------\nRule of Conditional Proof (CP)\nStatement\nIf, by assuming  𝑹  together with the given premises, we can derive  𝑺 , then we can conclude 𝑹→𝑺 from\nthe given premises alone.",
