    "t": "Problem 10)\nGiven statements:\nIf a student attends classes, then they understand the subject.\nIf a student understands the subject, then they pass the exam.\nThe student did not pass the exam.\nConclusion:\nThe student did not attend classes.\nSolution\nLet:\np: Student attends classes\nq: Student understands subject\nr: Student passes exam\nPremises:\n𝑝→𝑞\n𝑞→𝑟\n¬𝑟\nSteps:\n𝑝→𝑟\n(Hypothetical Syllogism)      ¬𝑝\n(Modus Tollens)\n\nConclusion: ¬𝑝\nDefinition\nA set of premises 𝐻ଵ, 𝐻ଶ, … , 𝐻௡ is said to be consistent if the conjunction 𝐻ଵ∧𝐻ଶ∧⋯∧𝐻௡ has\ntruth value True (T) in at least one possible interpretation.\nIn simple words:\nThere exists at least one assignment of truth values that makes all premises true simultaneously.\nCONSISTENCY OF PREMISES\nShow that the premises are consistent\nPremises:    P1:  𝑃∨𝑄      𝑃2: ¬𝑃\nWe check the conjunction:  𝑃∨𝑄∧¬𝑃\n(P∨Q)∧¬P\nP∨Q\n¬P\nQ\nP\nF\nT\nF\nT\nT\nF\nT\nF\nF\nT\nT\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nT\nF\nF\nObservation\nThe conjunction is True when:\n𝑃= 𝐹, 𝑄= 𝑇\nTherefore,\n𝑃∨𝑄∧¬𝑃\nis true in at least one situation.\nConclusion\nThe premises are consistent.\nDefinition\nA set of premises  𝐻ଵ, 𝐻ଶ, … , 𝐻௡is said to be inconsistent if the conjunction 𝐻ଵ∧𝐻ଶ∧⋯∧𝐻௡is\nFalse (F) in all possible interpretations.\nMeaning:\nThere is no possible situation where all premises are true together.\nINCONSISTENCY OF PREMISES",
