    "p": 59,
    "t": "Example 2: Show that the premises are inconsistent\nPremises:  P1: ¬𝑃∧𝑄\nP2: 𝑃\nCheck conjunction: ¬𝑃∧𝑄∧𝑃\n(¬P∧Q)∧P\n¬P∧Q\n¬P\nQ\nP\nF\nF\nF\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nF\nT\nF\nT\nT\nT\nF\nF\nF\nT\nF\nF\nObservation\nFinal column is False in all rows.\nConclusion\nThe premises are inconsistent.\nShow that:\n(i) 𝑃→𝑄,   𝑃→𝑅,   𝑄→¬𝑅,  𝑅are consistent.\n(ii) 𝑃→𝑄,   𝑃→𝑅,   𝑄→¬𝑅,  𝑃are inconsistent.\nLet:\n𝐴= 𝑃→𝑄∧𝑃→𝑅∧𝑄→¬𝑅∧𝑅\n𝐵= 𝑃→𝑄∧𝑃→𝑅∧𝑄→¬𝑅∧𝑃\nAfter constructing the truth table:\nExpression A has at least one True value.\nExpression B is False in all cases.\nConclusion\nSet (i) is consistent.\nSet (ii) is inconsistent.\nDerivation Method Example\nShow that the following premises are inconsistent:      𝑃→𝑄,   𝑄→𝑅,   𝑆→¬𝑅,   𝑃∧𝑆\nStep-by-Step Derivation\nGiven premises:\n1. 𝑃∧𝑆\n(Premise)\n2. 𝑃\n(from 1)\n3. 𝑆\n(from 1)\n4. 𝑃→𝑄\n(Premise)\n5. 𝑄\n(from 2,4)\n6. 𝑄→𝑅\n(Premise)\n7. 𝑅\n(from 5,6)\n8. 𝑆→¬𝑅\n(Premise)\n9. ¬𝑅\n(from 3,8)\n10. 𝑅∧¬𝑅\n(contradiction)\n11. 𝐹\nFinal Conclusion\nSince we derived a contradiction,            The premises are inconsistent.\nIntroduction\nSo far our discussion was limited to propositional logic, where:\nWe considered whole statements.  Each statement was either True or False.\nSymbols like 𝑃, 𝑄, 𝑅, 𝑃ଵ, 𝑃ଶrepresented complete statements.\nThe inference theory was restricted in the sense that:\nPremises were statements.\nConclusions were statements.\nNo internal structure of statements was analyzed.\nHowever, propositional logic cannot express relationships such as:\n“Any two objects have some common property.”\nTo investigate such statements, we introduce the concept of predicates.\nDefinition\nThe logic based upon the analysis of predicates in statements is called Predicate Logic (or\nPredicate Calculus).\nPredicate logic studies the internal structure of statements.\nPREDICATE CALCULUS",
