    "p": 60,
    "t": "Example 1:\nConsider the statement:    “x is greater than 10.”\nThis statement has two parts:     Subject: x                Predicate: “is greater than 10”\nThe predicate describes a property of the subject.\nWe denote this as:       𝑷𝒙\nWhere:        𝑷     represents the predicate “is greater than 10”\n          𝒙\nis the variable\nP(x) is called a propositional function.\nIt becomes a proposition only after assigning a value to 𝒙.\nPREDICATE AND PROPOSITIONAL FUNCTION\nExample:\n•𝑃15\n•𝑃5\nExample:\n•𝑃15 15 :is greater than 10 →TRUE\n•𝑃5 5 :is greater than 10 →FALSE\nNote:\nThe subject is also called:\n•Object\n•Individual\n•Variable\nExample:\n“x is taller than y”  Here:  x and y are subjects     “is taller than” is the predicate\nDenoted as:   𝑇𝑥𝑦\nThis is a 2-place predicate.\nTypes:\n1-place predicate →depends on 1 variable\n2-place predicate →depends on 2 variables\nm-place predicate →depends on m variables\nExamples:\nJohn is a bachelor →𝑃𝐽)\n- place)\nPavan and Shanker played carroms against Arjun and Charan →𝐹𝑃𝑆𝐴𝐶\n-\n-\nplace)\nRevanth had lunch with Rama and Ravi →𝑃𝑥𝑦𝑧\n- place)\nTelangana is neighbour of Andhra →𝑃𝑇𝐴\n-\nplace)\nMULTI-PLACE PREDICATES\nImportant Rule:\nIf 𝑆  is  an n-place predicate and 𝑎ଵ, 𝑎ଶ, . . . , 𝑎௡are\nobjects, then:\n𝑆𝑎ଵ𝑎ଶ. . . 𝑎௡\nis a statement.\nPredicates can be combined using logical connectives.\nExample:\nLet\n𝐵𝐽 :John is a bachelor\n𝑅𝑃 :This painting is red\nThen:\nJohn is a bachelor AND painting is red    is          𝐵𝐽∧𝑅𝑃\nJohn is a bachelor OR painting is red  is               𝐵𝐽∨𝑅𝑃\nIf John is a bachelor then painting is red   is      𝐵𝐽→𝑅𝑃\nCOMPOUND STATEMENTS IN PREDICATE LOGIC\nSome statements involve quantity words such as:\nAll\nSome\nEvery\nNo one\nThere exists\nThese words are called Quantifiers.\nThere are two types: Universal Quantifier    and   Existential Quantifier\n(i) Universal Quantifier    -\nSymbol:   ∀\nMeaning:\nFor all\nFor every\nFor each\nEverything\nExamples:\nAll birds have wings\nFor every integer x, x² ≥ 0\n∀𝑥𝑥ଶ≥0\nQUANTIFIERS IN PREDICATE LOGIC\nii) Existential Quantifier         - Symbol:      ∃\n•\nMeaning:\n•\nThere exists\n•\nThere is at least one\n•\nSome\nExample:\n•\nThere exists a real number whose square equals itself.\n•\n∃𝑥𝑥ଶ= 𝑥",
