    "p": 61,
    "t": "Let:     𝐺𝑥 :x is good\n(a) Something is good    - ∃𝒙𝑮𝒙\n(b) Everything is good    - ∀𝒙𝑮𝒙\n(c) Nothing is good   - ∀𝒙¬𝑮𝒙\n(d) Something is not good   - ∃𝒙¬𝑮𝒙\nTRANSLATION EXAMPLES\nLet:     𝑀𝑥 :x is a man            𝐺𝑥 :x is good\n1. All men are good      − ∀𝒙𝑴𝒙→𝑮𝒙\n(Rule so )\nIf someone is a man, then that person must be good.\n2. No men are good       − ∀𝒙𝑴𝒙→¬𝑮𝒙\nIf someone is a man, then that person is not good.\n3. Some men are good    − ∃𝒙𝑴𝒙∧𝑮𝒙\n(both so ^)\nThere exists at least one person who is both\na man AND good.\n4. Some men are not good    − ∃𝒙𝑴𝒙∧¬𝑮𝒙\nThere exists at least one person who\nis a man  AND is NOT good.\nTo negate:  Change quantifier:     ∀\n∃\nNegate the predicate.\nRules:\n¬ ∀𝑥𝑃𝑥\n≡∃𝑥¬𝑃𝑥\n¬ ∃𝑥𝑃𝑥\n≡∀𝑥¬𝑃𝑥\nExample:\nAll equilateral triangles are isosceles.\nLet:       𝑇𝑥 :x is equilateral           𝐼𝑥 :x is isosceles\nStatement:    ∀𝑥𝑇𝑥→𝐼𝑥\nNegation:      ∃𝑥𝑇𝑥∧¬𝐼𝑥\nNEGATION OF QUANTIFIED STATEMENTS\nA variable is:\nFree →if not bound by quantifier\nBound →if controlled by a quantifier\nScope\nThe part of the formula to which a quantifier applies is called its scope.\nExample:                             ∀𝑥𝑃𝑥, 𝑦\n𝑥is bound          & 𝑦is free\nFREE AND BOUND VARIABLES\nRule US (Universal Specification)\nFrom:           ∀𝑥𝑃𝑥\nWe can conclude:                      𝑃𝑎                          for any object a.\nRule ES (Existential Specification)\nFrom:            ∃𝑥𝑃𝑥                There exists some element a such that:                𝑃𝑎\nRule UG (Universal Generalization)\nIf 𝑃𝑎is true for arbitrary a:              ∀𝑥𝑃𝑥\nRule EG (Existential Generalization)\nIf 𝑃𝑎is true for some a:                     ∃𝑥𝑃𝑥\nRULES OF INFERENCE IN PREDICATE LOGIC",
