    "p": 3,
    "t": "▪Ex:\n▪The set of all Canadians\n▪A pair of shoes\n▪A bouquet of flowers\n▪The set of all ideas contained in the book\n▪A flock of sheep\n▪A collection of rocks\n▪But are they relevant?\nIn maths – Set of triangles, set of real numbers, set of alphabets – common\nBut, a set containing {A Horse, the Letter A, a Jacket, Ms Bhargavi} - ???\nIs acceptable but not interesting and not important\nSET THEORY – WHAT ARE SETS?\nSET THEORY\n1. Vague / Ambiguous\nCollections\nExample: \"The collection of all tall\npeople in the city“\n\nProblem: “Tall” is subjective —\nwhat height counts?\nExample: \"The set of beautiful\npaintings“\n\nProblem: “Beautiful” is based on\nopinion.\nSET THEORY - WHAT ARE NOT A SET?\n2. Self-Referential / Paradoxical Collections\nExample: \"The set of all sets that are not members of themselves\n\"Known as Russell’s Paradox — leads to logical contradiction.\nSET THEORY - WHAT ARE NOT A SET?",
    "ocr": false
