    "p": 5,
    "t": "SET THEORY\n▪x ∊A\n▪x is an element of the set A\n▪x belongs to set A or\n▪x is in A\n▪x ∉A or (x ∊A)\n▪x is not an element of the set A\n▪x doesn’t belongs to set A or\n▪x is not in A\nROSTER FORM\nFINITE & INFINITE SETS\n▪Finite: In principle, any finite set can be defined by an explicit list of its\nmembers.\n▪Infinite: But specifying infinite sets requires a rule or pattern to indicate\nmembership\n▪For example, the ellipsis in {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, …} indicates that the list\nof natural numbers ‘ℕ’ goes on forever.\n▪Empty: The empty (or void, or null) set, symbolized by { } or Ø, contains\nno elements at all.\n▪Nonetheless, it has the status of being a set.",
