    "t": "SET THEORY: SUBSETS,\nEQUALITY, INCLUSION\nSUBSET / SUPERSET INCLUSION - DEFINITION\n▪Given two sets P and Q, we say that P is a Subset of Q or P is\nincluded in Q, if\n▪every element in P is also an element in Q.\n▪Notation: P ⊆Q and\n▪Example:\n▪{a, b} ⊆{y, x, b, c, a} (True)\n▪{a, b} ⊆{a, c, d, e} (False)\nSUBSET / SUPERSET INCLUSION - DEFINITION\n▪Definition: A set A is a Superset of set B if every element of B is also in A,\nwritten as A⊇B.\n▪Proper Superset: A⊃B if A has all elements of B and at least one extra element.\n▪Mathematical Example:\n▪A={1,2,3,4}, B={2,3}⇒ A⊃B and A⊇B\n▪Real-World Example (College Campus):\n▪A = All students in the college.\n▪B = Students in the Computer Science department.\n⇒ College students (A) are a superset of CS students (B).\nSUBSET – REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE\n▪Example: Subsets in a Library Context\n▪Let’s consider:\n▪A = Set of all fiction books in a library\n▪B = Set of all books in the library\n▪C = Set of all science fiction books in the library\n Statements:\n▪C ⊆ A: The set of all science fiction books is a subset of the set of all fiction books\n▪C ⊆ B: The set of all science fiction books is also a subset of the set of all books in the library\n▪A ⊆ B: The set of all fiction books is a subset of the set of all books\n Not True:\n▪A ⊆ C: Not all fiction books are science fiction, so this is not true\n▪B ⊆ A: Not all books are fiction; hence, the set of all books is not a subset of fiction books",
