    "p": 11,
    "t": "SUBSET – REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE\n▪Example: Subsets in the Context of Vehicles\n▪Let’s define the following sets:\n▪X = Set of all electric cars\n▪Y = Set of all cars\n▪Z = Set of all vehicles\n True Relationships:\n▪X ⊆ Y: Every electric car is a car, so the set of electric cars is a subset of the set of cars\n▪X ⊆ Z: Every electric car is also a vehicle, so it's a subset of the set of vehicles\n▪Y ⊆ Z: All cars are vehicles, so the set of cars is a subset of the set of vehicles\n Not True:\n▪Y ⊆ X: Not all cars are electric, so cars are not a subset of electric cars\n▪Z ⊆ Y: Not all vehicles are cars (e.g., motorcycles, trucks), so vehicles are not a subset of cars\nSUBSET VS ELEMENT\nLet\n▪A = {a, b, c} and\n▪B = {{a, b, c}, a, b, c}\nThen:\n▪A ∈B -> True, because the whole set A={a, b, c} is explicitly listed as an element inside B\n▪A ⊆B -> True, because every element of A — that is, a, b, and c — is also in B\n▪Remember:\n▪\n{4} ⊆{{}} →False\n▪The set on the right contains only the empty set ∅ as an element\n▪4 is not equal to ∅, so {4} is not a subset of {{}}\n▪\n{4} ∈{{}} →False\n▪{4} is not an element of {{}} — again, only the empty set is an element there\n▪\n∅⊆A (Always True)\n▪Always True, because the empty set is a subset of every set\n▪\n∅∈A (Depends on A)\n▪True if and only if the empty set is explicitly an element in A\n▪E.g., if A={∅,𝑎,𝑏} then\n▪If A={a, b}, then\nPROPERTIES OF SUBSETS\nReflexive Property of Set Inclusion\n▪Definition: Every set is a subset of itself.\nA ⊆ A\n▪Explanation:\nA set includes all its own elements.\nSo, it must be a subset of itself.\n▪Example:\nLet A = { 1, 2, 3 }\nThen, clearly: A ⊆ A (True, because all elements of A are in A)\nPROPERTIES OF SUBSETS\nTransitive Property of Set Inclusion\nDefinition:\n\nIf set A ⊆ B and B ⊆ C, then A ⊆ C\nExample:\n▪Let:\n▪A = {1, 2}\n▪B = {1, 2, 3}\n▪C = {1, 2, 3, 4}\nWe can see:\n▪A ⊆ B (True) & B ⊆ C (True)\n\nTherefore: A ⊆ C (True)\nReal-world Analogy:\n▪All apples are fruits (A ⊆ B)\n▪All fruits are grocery items (B ⊆ C)\n▪Therefore, all apples are grocery items (A ⊆ C)",
