    "t": "EQUALITY OF SETS\n▪Two sets P and Q are equal if they contain exactly the same elements,\nregardless of:\n▪How they are defined,\n▪The order of elements, Or\n▪Whether some elements are repeated.\nMathematically:\n\nP = Q ⟺ P ⊆ Q and Q ⊆ P\nEQUALITY OF SETS\nExample:\n▪P = {x | x is an even positive integer ≤ 10}\n▪Q = {x | x = y + z, y, z ∈ {1, 3, 5}}\n▪Then P = Q (both contain: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10)\nIn particular, it does not matter how the set is defined or denoted.\n▪For example: The set {1, 2, 3, 4} =\n\n{x | x is an integer where x>0 and x<5 } =\n\n{x | x is a positive integer whose square is  >0 and <25}\ny\nz\ny + z\nEQUALITY OF SETS\nReal-World Example 1:\nSame Basket of Fruits\nSet P: A fruit basket that has: {apple, banana, orange}\nSet Q: A second fruit basket filled by saying:\n\n“Pick any fruit from the set of {banana, orange, apple}”\nEven though the order or description is different, both baskets have the same\nfruits.\n Therefore,\n\nP=Q\nEQUALITY OF SETS\nReal-World Example 2:\nSet P: All students whose names are in the attendance list:\n\n{Alice, Bob, Charlie, David}\nSet Q: All students with roll numbers 1 to 4 in Section A:\nAssume:\n\nRoll 1 → Alice\n\nRoll 2 → Bob\n\nRoll 3 → Charlie\n\nRoll 4 → David\nSo both P and Q have exactly the same people, just defined differently.\n Therefore,\n\nP = Q",
