    "t": "▪Power Set P(S) of a set S is the set of all subsets of S.\n▪For example S = {x, y, z} then\nP(s) = {\n{ },\n{x}, {y}, {z},\n{x, y}, {x, z}, {y, z},\n{x, y, z}\n}\n▪If S has n elements in it then P(s) will have 2n elements\nPOWER SET\n▪Power Set of the null set ∅ has only the element ∅;\n▪Hence P(∅ ) = {∅ }.\n▪For a set S1 = {a},\nThe power set P(S1) = {∅ , {a}} = {∅ ,S1}\n▪For a set S2 = {a, b},\nP(S2) = {∅, {a}, {b}, S2}\nPOWER SET\n▪For a set S3 = {a, b, c},\n▪P(S3) = {∅, {a}, {b}, {c}, {a, b}, {b, c}, {a, c}, (a, b, c}}\n▪P(S3) = {∅, {a}, {b}, {c}, {a, b}, {b, c}, {a, c}, S3}\nThe number of elements in P(S) is:\n|P(S)| = 2^n\nPOWER SET",
