    "p": 29,
    "t": "▪The Cartesian product of sets is not limited to only two sets. It also holds\nfor more than two sets. But the complexity increases as we increase the\nnumber of sets.\n▪For three sets A, B and C, an element of A ×B × C is represented as (a, b,\nc) and it is called an ordered triplet.\n▪If we take the Cartesian product of two sets as, R × R where R is the set of\nreal numbers, that represents the entire two-dimensional Cartesian\nplane. Similarly, R × R × R represents three-dimensional Cartesian\nspace.\nK – FOLD PRODUCT\n▪For any non – empty sets A1 , A2, ...., Ak, the k-fold product A1 x A2 x .... x Ak is\ndefined as the set of all ordered k-tuples (a1, a2, ...., ak), where ai  Ai, i = 1, 2, … ,\nk.\nA1 x A2 x … x Ak = {(a1, a2, ...., ak), | ai  Ai, i = 1, 2, … , k }\nFor Ex = if A = {1,0}, B={2,-2}, C={0,-1} then A x B x C = ?\n▪As with the ordered pairs, if (a1, a2, ...., ak), (b1, b2, ...., bk) are k-tuples,\nthen\n(a1, a2, ...., ak) = (b1, b2, ...., bk)\nif and only if\nai = bi\nFor i = 1, 2, …k\n▪The cardinality of |A1 x A2 x .... x Ak | = | A1 | x | A2 | x .... x | Ak |\n▪Find x and y:\ni. (2x, x+y) = (6,1)\nii. (y-2, 2x+1) = (x-1, y+2)\n▪Let A={1,3,5}, B={2,3} and C={4,6} – Solve\n▪AxB\n▪BxA\n▪BxC\n▪AxC\n▪(A x B)  C\n▪(A  B) x C\n▪A  (B x C)\n▪A  (B x C)\n▪(A x B)  (B x C)\n▪(A x B)  (B x A)\n▪(A x B)  (B x C)\nEXERCISE",
