    "t": "▪Predicate logic and set theory are deeply connected.\n▪Whenever we say:    ∀𝑥𝑃𝑥\nor               ∃𝑥𝑃𝑥    we are usually talking about elements of a set.\n Universal Quantifier over a Set\nInstead of writing:  ∀𝑥𝑃𝑥\nwe often write:   ∀𝑥∈𝐴, 𝑃𝑥\n▪This means:    For every element 𝑥belonging to set A, property P(𝑥) holds.\nExample 1\n▪Let A = {2,4,6,8}\n▪Statement:   ∀𝑥∈𝐴, 𝑥 is even          This is TRUE.\nExample 2\n▪Let:    A = ℕ (natural numbers)\n▪Statement :         ∀𝑥∈ℕ, 𝑥2 ≥0\nThis is TRUE.\nUSING SET WITH QUANTIFIERS\nExistential Quantifier over a Set\n∃𝑥∈𝐴, 𝑃𝑥   means:           There exists at least one element in A satisfying P(x).\nExample\n▪Let A = ℤ (integers)         ∃𝑥∈ℤ, 𝑥2 = 4\nTrue (x = 2 or -2)\nTranslating between Sets and Quantifiers\n▪𝐴⊆𝐵             means:              ∀𝑥𝑥∈𝐴→𝑥∈𝐵\n▪𝐴= 𝐵\nmeans:              ∀𝑥𝑥∈𝐴\n𝑥∈𝐵\n▪A ∩ B = ∅       𝐦𝐞𝐚𝐧s:\n∀𝑥¬ 𝑥∈𝐴∧𝑥∈𝐵\nRUSSELS PARADOX\n▪Normally:   𝐴∪𝐵\nmeans elements in A or B.\n▪But what if we have many sets?\n Indexed Family of Sets\n▪Suppose we have a family:       ቄ𝐴𝑖}𝑖∈𝐼\n▪Meaning:        For each index i in set I, there is a set Aᵢ.\n Generalized Union\nራ\n𝑖∈𝐼\n𝐴𝑖\n▪Definition:        𝑥∈ሪ𝑖∈𝐼𝐴𝑖\n⟺∃𝑖∈𝐼such that 𝑥∈𝐴𝑖\n▪So union corresponds to existential quantifier.\nGENERALIZED UNIONS AND INTERACTIONS\nExample\nLet:\n𝐴𝑛= 𝑛𝑛+ 1\nThen:\nራ\n𝑛=1\n𝐴𝑛= {1, 2, 3, 4}",
