    "t": "▪Let A = {1,2,3,4}, B={w,x,y,z}, C = {5,6,7}\n▪R1 = {(1,x),(2,x),(3,y),(3,z)}\n▪R2 = {(w,5),(x,6)}\n▪R3={(w,5),(w,6)}\n▪Draw\n▪R1o R2\n▪R1o R3\n▪M(R1)\n▪M(R2)\n▪M(R3)\nEX\n▪M(R1o R2)\n▪M(R1) . M(R2)\n▪Check if M(R1o R2)  = M(R1) . M(R2)\nComplement of a Relation\n▪The complement of R is defined as Rc = (A × A) − R.\n▪Example: For A = {1, 2}, R = {(1, 1)}, Rc = {(1, 2), (2, 1), (2, 2)}.\nOther Types of Relations\n▪Apart from the property-based relations like reflexive or symmetric, relations can also be classified\naccording to how elements from one set are mapped to elements of another set.\nEX\n▪Let A = {1,2,3}. Determine the nature of the following relations on A:\n▪R1 = { (1,2), (2,1),(1,3) (3,1)}\n▪R2 = {(1,1)(2,2)(3,3)(2,3)}\n▪R3 = {(1,1),(2,2)(3,3)}\n▪R4 = {(1,1)(2,2)(3,3)(2,3)(3,2)\n▪R5 = {(1,1)(2,3)(3,3)}\n▪R6 = {(2,3)(3,4)(2,4)}\n▪R7 = {(1,3)(3,2)}\n▪R1 is symmetric and irreflexive, not transitive\n▪R2 is reflexive and transitive but not symmetric\n▪R3 and R4 are both reflexive and symmetric\n▪R5 is neither reflexive nor symmetric\n▪R6 is transitive and irreflexive but not symmetric\n▪R7 is irreflexive not transitive not symmetric\nTransitive Closure\n▪Let  A be a set and  R be a relation on  A.  The transitive closure of  R, denoted by  R+, is the\nsmallest transitive relation that contains  R as a subset.\n▪If  R is a relation on a set  A and |A|=n, then the transitive closure of  R is the union of the first\nn powers of r.\n▪That is,\nR+ = R ∪ R2 ∪ R3 ∪ ⋯∪ Rn.\nA = { 1, 2, 3}, R= { (1,2), (2,3), (3,1) }, R+\n = ?\nCLOSURE PROPERTIES",
