    "t": "Example1:\nShow whether the relation (x, y) ∈ R, if, x ≥ y defined on the set of +ve integers is\na partial order relation.\nSolution: Consider the set A = {1, 2, 3, 4} containing four +ve integers.\nFind the relation for this set such as\nR = {(2, 1), (3, 1), (3, 2), (4, 1), (4, 2), (4, 3), (1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3), (4, 4)}.\n▪Reflexive:  The relation is reflexive as for every a ∈ A. (a, a) ∈ R,\n▪i.e. (1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3), (4, 4) ∈ R.\n▪Antisymmetric:  The relation is antisymmetric as whenever (a, b) and (b, a) ∈ R, we have a = b.\n▪Transitive: The relation is transitive as whenever (a, b) and (b, c) ∈ R, we have (a, c) ∈ R.\nExample: (4, 2) ∈ R and (2, 1) ∈ R, implies (4, 1) ∈ R.\n▪As the relation is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive.\n▪Hence, it is a partial order relation.\nExample2:\n▪Show that the relation 'Divides' defined on N is a partial order relation.\nSolution:\n▪Reflexive: We have a divides a, ∀ a∈N.\n▪Therefore, relation 'Divides' is reflexive.\n▪Antisymmetric: Let a, b ∈ N, such that a divides b. It implies b divides a iff a = b.\n▪So, the relation is antisymmetric.\n▪Transitive: Let a, b, c ∈ N, such that a divides b and b divides c. Then a divides  c.\n▪Hence the relation is transitive.\n▪Thus, the relation being reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive, the relation 'divides' is a\npartial order relation.\nPARTIAL ORDER VS TOTAL ORDER\n1. Comparability in a Poset\n▪A poset is a set with a relation “≼” (like ≤) that tells us how elements are related.\n▪Two elements a and b are called comparable if we can say either a comes before b (a ≼ b)\nor b comes before a (b ≼ a).\nNot Comparable (inside a poset)\nConsider the set 𝑆=\n1 2\n2 1\nwith the relation\n“≤” defined component-wise (product order):\n𝑎𝑏≤𝑐𝑑\n⟺𝑎≤𝑐and 𝑏≤𝑑\n Compare (1,2) and (2,1):\n•(1,2) ≤ (2,1)? No, because 2 ≤ 1 is false.\n•(2,1) ≤ (1,2)? No, because 1 ≤ 2 is true but 2 ≤ 1 is\nfalse.\nSo these two are not comparable.\n This shows that not all posets are chains.\n Think of two tasks:\nTask A must be done before Task B, or\nTask B must be done before Task A.\nIf one of these is true, the tasks are\ncomparable.\n Example:\nIn numbers: 3 ≤ 7, so 3 and 7 are\ncomparable.\nIn tasks: “Submit homework” must be done\nbefore “Get grade.” Comparable.",
