    "t": "1. Minimal Element\n▪An element 𝑚 is minimal if no other element is strictly smaller\nthan it in the poset.\n▪There can be many minimal elements.\n Symbol:\n▪Minimal: ∄𝑥∈𝑆\n𝑥≺𝑚\nExample (Hasse diagram of subsets of 1 2 )\n▪Here, { } is the only least element.\n▪But if we consider the middle level: both {1} and {2} are\nminimal because nothing is below them (except { } which is\nthe least).\nMAXIMAL, MINIMAL, GREATEST, LEAST\n2. Maximal Element\n▪An element 𝑚 is maximal if no other element is strictly larger than it\nin the poset.\n▪There can be many maximal elements.\n Symbol:\n▪Maximal: ∄x ∈ S (m ≺ x)\nExample (Hasse diagram of subsets of 1 2 )\n▪{1,2} is a greatest element . If we remove {1,2}, then {1} and\n{2} become maximal elements.\nMAXIMAL, MINIMAL, GREATEST, LEAST..\n3. Least Element\n▪An element 𝑙 is the least element if it is smaller than or equal to every\nelement in the poset.\n▪There can be only one least element (if it exists).\n Symbol:\n ∀x ∈ S, l⪯x\nExample (Hasse diagram of subsets of 1 2 )\n▪In the diagram above, { } is the least element.\nMAXIMAL, MINIMAL, GREATEST,\nLEAST..",
