    "t": "Step 1: Look at one question\nEach question has 5 choices.\nSo:\n5 ways\nStep 2: There are 15 independent questions\nFor every question, there are 5 choices.\nTherefore:\n5 × 5 × 5 × ⋯× 5\n15 times.\n5ଵହ\n30,517,578,125\nAnswer:\n30,517,578,125\n(a) Six different commercials, each shown once\nWe have 6 different commercials:\n𝐴, 𝐵, 𝐶, 𝐷, 𝐸, 𝐹\nAll 6 are arranged in 6 slots.\nOrder matters.\nTherefore:\n଺𝑃଺= 6!\n= 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1\nAnswer:\n(b) Three different commercials, each shown\ntwice\nSuppose the commercials are:    𝐴, 𝐴, 𝐵, 𝐵, 𝐶, 𝐶\nThere are 6 positions.\nIf all were different:   6!\nBut the two A's are identical, the two B's are identical,\nand the two C's are identical.\nTherefore divide by:    2! 2!2!\nHence:\n6!\n2!2! 2!\n= 720\nAnswer: 90\n(c) Two different commercials, each shown three\ntimes\nSuppose:\n𝐴, 𝐴, 𝐴, 𝐵, 𝐵, 𝐵\nTotal = 6 slots.\nNumber of arrangements:\n6!\n3! 3!\n= 720\n6 × 6\n= 720\nAnswer: 20\n(d) Three commercials shown 3, 2 and 1 times\nSuppose:\n𝐴, 𝐴, 𝐴, 𝐵, 𝐵, 𝐶\nTotal:\n3 + 2 + 1 = 6\nNumber of arrangements:\n6!\n3! 2! 1!\n= 720\n6 × 2\n= 60\nAnswer:\nImportant idea\nWhenever objects are repeated/identical,\nuse: n1 !n2 !⋯n!",
