    "t": "Q15. True-False test\nThere are 20 questions.\nEach question has: 2 choices\nTrue or False.\n(a) Every question is answered\nEach of the 20 questions has 2 choices.\nTherefore:\n2ଶ଴= 1,048,576\nAnswer: 1,048,576\n(b) 7 correct and 13 incorrect\nThere are 20 questions.\nWe need to choose which 7 questions are correct.\nOnce those 7 are selected, the other 13 are automatically incorrect.\nTherefore:\nଶ଴𝐶଻\n= 20!\n7! 13!\n77,520\nAnswer: 77,520\n(c) 10 correct and 10 incorrect\nChoose the 10 questions that are correct:\nଶ଴𝐶ଵ଴\n=\n20!\n10! 10!\n184,756\nAnswer: 184,756\nKey idea\nIf the question says exactly 𝑟correct, use: nCr\nQ16. Seven nominees\nThere are:\n•\n3 men\n•\n4 women\nTotal: 7\nTwo vacancies must be filled.\n(a) Any two nominees\nChoose 2 from 7:\n଻𝐶ଶ\n= 7!\n2!5!\n= 7 × 6\nAnswer: 21\n(b) Any two women\nThere are 4 women.\nChoose 2:\nସ𝐶ଶ\n= 4!\n2! 2!\n= 6\nAnswer: 6\n(c) One man and one woman\nChoose:\n1 man from 3:\nଷ𝐶ଵ= 3\n1 woman from 4:\nସ𝐶ଵ= 4\nMultiply:\n3 × 4\nAnswer:\nQ17. Clothes for a trip\nMr. Jones has:\n•\n4 pairs of pants\n•\n7 shirts\n•\n3 sweaters\nHe chooses:\n•\n2 pants\n•\n3 shirts\n•\n1 sweater\nSince order does not matter, use combinations.\nPants:\nସ𝐶ଶ= 6\nShirts:\n଻𝐶ଷ=\n଻!\nଷ!ସ! = 35\nSweaters:\nଷ𝐶ଵ= 3\nMultiply:\nସ𝐶ଶ×\n଻𝐶ଷ×\nଷ𝐶ଵ= 6 × 35 × 3 = 630\nAnswer:630\nQ18. Distribute A, B, B, B, C, C, F\nWe have 7 students.\nThe grades are:\n•\nA →1\n•\nB →3\n•\nC →2\n•\nF →1\nSo we are arranging:\n𝐴, 𝐵, 𝐵, 𝐵, 𝐶, 𝐶, 𝐹\nThe B's are identical and C's are identical.\nTherefore:\n7!\n1! 3! 2! 1!\n= 5040\n6 × 2\n= 5040\nAnswer:\nImportant\nThis is not 7!, because the three B's are identical and the\ntwo C's are identical.\nQ19. Ten original paintings to three heirs\nThere are:\n10 different paintings\nand\n3 heirs\nEach painting can be left to any one of the 3\nheirs.\nFor each painting:\n3 choices\nFor 10 paintings:\n3 × 3 × ⋯× 3\n10 times.\nTherefore:\n3ଵ଴\n59,049\nAnswer:\n59,049\nQ20. Baseball tickets\nThe fan has tickets for:\n6 different games\nHe has:\n5 friends\nFor each game, he can choose any one of the 5 friends.\nSo:\nGame 1:\n5 choices\nGame 2:\n5 choices\nand so on.\nTherefore:\n5଺\n= 5 × 5 × 5 × 5 × 5 × 5\n15,625\nAnswer:\n15,625\nA friend can be taken to more than one game, so we do\nnot use 5𝑃଺.",
