    "p": 20,
    "t": "(e) There are exactly 3 fives\nThere are four 5's in a deck:\n5 ,   5 ,   5 ,   5\nWe need exactly 3 fives.\nStep 1: Choose 3 fives from 4\nସ𝐶ଷ\n= 4\nStep 2: Choose the remaining 2 cards\nThere are 52 cards in total.\nWe have already selected 3 fives.\nBut we must make sure the remaining cards are not\nfives, otherwise we would have 4 fives.\nSo available non-five cards:\n52 −4 = 48\nChoose 2 from these 48:\nସ଼𝐶ଶ\n= 48 × 47\n= 1128\nStep 3: Multiply\nସ𝐶ଷ×\nସ଼𝐶ଶ\n= 4 × 1128\n= 4512\nAnswer: 4512\n(f) There are exactly 2 Queens and exactly 2 hearts\nThis is the most important one because Queen of\nHearts belongs to BOTH groups.\nWe need:\n{Exactly 2 Queens AND exactly 2 Hearts}}\nThere are:\n•\n4 Queens\n•\n13 Hearts\n•\nQueen of Hearts (Q ) is common to both.\nTherefore, we have to consider two cases.\nCase 1: Queen of Hearts IS selected\nSuppose: [ Q\n]\nis one of our cards.\nWe already have:\n•\n1 Queen\n•\n1 Heart\nWe need:\n•\n1 more Queen\n•\n1 more Heart\n•\n1 additional card\nChoose the second Queen\nThere are 3 Queens left:\n[ Q , Q ,Q\n]\nTherefore: [ 3C1=3]\nChoose the second Heart\nThere are 13 hearts, but (Q ) has already been selected.\nSo there are: [ 13-1=12] other hearts.\nChoose 1: [12C1=12]\nChoose the fifth card\nThis card must be:\n•\nnot a Queen\n•\nnot a Heart\nHow many such cards are there?\nThere are 52 total cards.\nCards that are Queen or Heart:\n\\[ 4+13-1=16 \\]\nWe subtract 1 because \\(Q \\) was counted in both\ngroups.\nTherefore cards that are neither Queen nor Heart:\n\\[ 52-16=36 \\]\nChoose 1:\n\\[ {}^{36}C_1=36 \\]\nTherefore Case 1:\n\\[ {}^3C_1\\times{}^{12}C_1\\times{}^{36}C_1 \\]\\[\n=3\\times12\\times36 \\]\\[ =\\boxed{1296} \\]\nAn expression consisting of two terms, connected by + or – sign is called a binomial expression.\nExamples of binomial expressions\nBINOMIAL THEOREM\nLet’s look at the expansion of  (x + y)n\n(x + y)0 = 1\n(x + y)1 = x + y\n(x + y)2 = x2 +2xy + y2\n(x + y)3 = x3 + 3x2y + 3xy2 + y3\n(x + y)4 = x4 + 4x3y + 6x2y2 + 4xy3 + y4\nBINOMIAL THEOREM",
