    "t": "Q. There are 367 people in a room.\nShow that at least two people have the same birthday.\nReasoning:\nThere are 366 possible birthdays (including Feb 29).\nWe have 367 people (pigeons) and 366 days (holes).\nBy the pigeonhole principle, at least one birthday is shared by 2 or more people.\nQ. Show that in any group of 13 people, at least two have birthdays in the same month.\nReasoning:\nThere are 12 months (holes) and 13 people (pigeons).\nSince 13 > 12, at least one month must have 2 or more birthdays.\nQ. Among 6 numbers, show that two of them leave the same remainder when divided by 5.\nReasoning:\nPossible remainders = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 →5 holes\nNumbers = 6 pigeons\n→By PHP, at least two numbers fall in the same remainder category.\nQ. How many students do you need in a school to guarantee that there are at least 2 students who\nhave the same first two initials?\nA. There are 26 × 26 = 676 different possible sets of two initials that can be obtained using the\n26 letters A, B, C, ..., Z, so the number of students should be greater than 676. Thus, the minimum\nnumber of students is 677.\nQ. Calvin has 13 pairs of shoes thrown around in his closet. The light has gone out, and he is\nunable to determine which shoe is which.\nWhat is the minimum number of shoes he must pick in order to guarantee that he has a matching\npair among them?\nDetails and Assumptions:\nEach shoe that he removes is either a left or right shoe of one of the 13 pairs.\nThe correct answer is: 14",
