    "t": "Q. A group of statistics professors hold a meet-and-greet over dinner.\nNo new guests enter the dinner after it has begun, and no one leaves until the dinner has ended.\nShow that at least two professors shook the same number of distinct individuals' hands.\n(Hint:A professor cannot shake his or her own hand.)\nDetails and Assumptions:\nIf there are 𝑛professors, then each individual could have shaken hands with at most 𝑛−1 other\nindividuals. Furthermore, if there exists an individual who shook hands with 𝑛−1 other\nindividuals, then there does not exist an individual who shook hands for no other individuals and\nvice versa.\nIn other words, each of the 𝑛professors can be thought of as an object to be sorted into one of 𝑛\n−1 boxes by the number of distinct individuals' hands shaken. By the pigeonhole principle, there\nexists at least one integer between 1 and 𝑛−1 or 0 and 𝑛−2 that equals the number of distinct\nindividuals' hands shaken by at least two professors.\nQ. Missy and Mussy are very messy sisters.\nTheir dresser drawer consists of 43 white socks, 2 black socks, 23 blue socks and 8 red socks.\nWhat is the minimum number of socks they must remove from the drawer, in order to be\ncertain that they have removed four socks of the same color?\nThe correct answer is: 12\n11 socks isn't enough as they could get 3 white, 2 black, 3 blue, 3 red socks.\n12 is enough, since they may pick at most 2 black socks, so they will have at least 10 white, blue\nor red socks.\nBy the pigeonhole principle, let the pigeons be socks and the pigeonholes be the color of the\nsocks (white, blue or red).\n𝟏𝟎> 𝟑× 𝟑so there are at least 4 socks of the same color.\nIf 𝑛objects are placed into 𝑚 boxes,\nthen at least one box contains at least\n𝑛\n𝑚\nobjects.\nHere, 𝑥means “round up to the nearest integer”.\nTHE GENERALIZED PIGEONHOLE PRINCIPLE\nIf you distribute 100 apples into 9 baskets,\nthen one basket has at least\n= 11.11. . .\n= 12\napples.\nSo, at least one basket has 12 or more apples.",
